QB.add(44,[{"id":44001,"t":"What Is Inflammation","s":"A student argues that inflammation is a targeted reaction that only starts once the body has identified the exact microbe involved. Which statement best corrects this?","o":["Inflammation is the outcome of innate immune activation, so it is immediate and non-specific","Inflammation is the outcome of adaptive immune activation, so it needs several days of priming first","Inflammation only occurs with infection, so sterile tissue damage does not trigger it","Inflammation is specific to the stressor because each pathogen has its own inflammatory pathway in tissue","Inflammation begins only after lymphocytes have been presented with antigen by dendritic cells"],"e":"Inflammation is the body's response to an irritant, an infectious pathogen, tissue damage or a stressor. It is the outcome of activating the innate immune response. Because the innate response is immediate and non-specific, inflammation shares those properties. Adaptive immunity may be activated later, but it does not start the process.","w":["Inflammation comes from innate activation and does not wait for days of adaptive priming.","Tissue damage and irritants trigger inflammation without any infection being present.","The response is non-specific, so it does not use a separate pathway for each pathogen.","Antigen presentation helps activate adaptive immunity, but inflammation is already under way through innate responses."],"k":"Inflammation = outcome of innate immunity: immediate and non-specific.","rn":[["","Inflammation is the outcome of activating what?","Inflammation is the outcome of activating what?","Innate immune response"],["","What are the 2 key properties of inflammation?","What are the 2 key properties of inflammation?","Immediate; Non-specific"]]},{"id":44002,"t":"What Is Inflammation","s":"A patient has had a persistent inflammatory condition for several years and now has organ damage far from the original site, driven by circulating cytokines. Which category of inflammation best describes the distant effects?","o":["Systemic inflammation","Acute inflammation","Primary local inflammation","Granulomatous inflammation confined to the original site","Complete resolution with tissue replacement"],"e":"Systemic inflammation is a whole-body, cytokine-induced response that can follow chronic inflammation. It can lead to conditions such as cardiovascular disease. Its importance is that it can damage organs or tissues far from the initial injury, which is what this patient shows.","w":["Acute inflammation is an immediate response lasting a few days, not years of distant damage.","A local process would stay at the site and would not explain distant organ damage.","A granuloma confined to one site would not explain cytokine-driven damage elsewhere.","Resolution means the response has ended, which does not fit continuing organ damage."],"k":"Systemic inflammation is cytokine-induced and can damage organs far from the injury.","rn":[["","Systemic inflammation is what type of response?","Systemic inflammation is what type of response?","Cytokine-induced inflammatory response"],["","Systemic inflammation can lead to what condition?","Systemic inflammation can lead to what condition?","Cardiovascular disease"]]},{"id":44003,"t":"What Is Inflammation","s":"Inflammation at a wound has now lasted ten days without settling. On the time course of inflammation, how should this be interpreted?","o":["It has passed the expected duration of acute inflammation and may be developing into chronic inflammation","It is still normal acute inflammation, because acute inflammation typically lasts for several months","It must already be systemic inflammation, because any response past three days becomes systemic","It cannot be inflammation, because inflammation always ends within twenty-four hours of injury","It is the repair phase only, since all inflammatory activity ends before day seven in every wound"],"e":"Acute inflammation is immediate, lasts a few days and should not last more than a week. Chronic inflammation develops if inflammation is prolonged beyond a week, sometimes for months or years. Ten days therefore points away from an uncomplicated acute response. Systemic inflammation is defined by whole-body cytokine effects, not simply by a time cut-off.","w":["Acute inflammation should not last longer than about a week, so months is far outside its course.","Systemic inflammation is a whole-body cytokine response, not any response beyond three days.","Acute inflammation lasts a few days, so it is not limited to twenty-four hours.","A persisting inflammatory state can overlap with repair, so inflammation does not always end by day seven."],"k":"Acute: days, under a week. Beyond a week: chronic.","rn":[["","How long does acute inflammation last?","How long does acute inflammation last?","A few days; Should not exceed a week"]]},{"id":44004,"t":"What Is Inflammation","s":"A patient's finger is red, hot and swollen around a splinter, and they can no longer bend it fully. Which of the five local signs is most directly explained by more severe damage rather than by the vascular changes?","o":["Functio laesa","Rubor","Calor","Tumor","The redness from increased flow"],"e":"Rubor (redness) reflects increased blood flow, calor (heat) reflects the temperature rise at the site, and tumor (swelling) reflects fluid accumulation. Functio laesa is reduction or loss of tissue or organ function and is seen if damage is more severe. The inability to bend the finger fits this sign.","w":["Rubor is redness caused by increased blood flow.","Calor is the rise in temperature at the site of injury.","Tumor is swelling caused by fluid accumulating at the site.","Redness is due to increased flow, which is a vascular change rather than loss of function."],"k":"Functio laesa = loss of function, when damage is more severe.","rn":[["","What are the 5 local signs of inflammation?","What are the 5 local signs of inflammation?","Redness (rubor); Heat (calor); Swelling (tumor); Pain (dolor); Loss of function (functio laesa)"],["","When does loss of function (functio laesa) occur?","When does loss of function (functio laesa) occur?","When the injury is more severe"]]},{"id":44005,"t":"What Is Inflammation","s":"Which set of findings most strongly suggests that an inflammatory response has developed a <b>systemic</b> component rather than remaining purely local?","o":["Fever, tiredness and feeling generally unwell","Redness, heat and swelling over the affected joint","Pain at the site with reduced movement of that limb","Localised swelling, with fluid accumulating at the site of a skin wound","A hot, tender, reddened area of skin next to a recent graze"],"e":"Local signs in the tissue are redness, heat, swelling, pain and loss of function. Tiredness, a general feeling of being unwell and fever are whole-body signs. They appear because a systemic component of inflammation is going on. Only the fever group therefore points beyond the site.","w":["These are the local tissue signs of rubor, calor and tumor.","Pain and reduced function at the site are local signs.","Fluid accumulation at the wound is the local sign of swelling.","Heat, tenderness and redness next to a graze remain local signs."],"k":"Fever, tiredness and malaise indicate a systemic component.","rn":[["","What are the 3 whole body signs of inflammation?","What are the 3 whole body signs of inflammation?","Tiredness; Feeling generally unwell; Fever"]]},{"id":44006,"t":"What Is Inflammation","s":"A diagnosis on a chart ends in the suffix \"-itis\". Which conclusion is most reasonable from the name alone?","o":["The disease is associated with inflammation of the named tissue","The disease is caused by a bacterial infection of the named tissue","The disease is a chronic disorder that has lasted more than a week","The disease has spread and now includes a systemic inflammatory component throughout the body","The tissue has already undergone necrosis because of restricted blood flow"],"e":"Diseases associated with inflammation often end in \"-itis\", such as dermatitis, bronchitis, otitis media, cystitis and meningitis. The suffix indicates inflammation, not its cause, its duration or its spread. Inflammation can arise from infection, chemicals, radiation, trauma, necrosis or an excessive immune reaction.","w":["Inflammation has many causes, so the name alone does not prove bacterial infection.","The suffix does not state whether the process is acute or chronic.","The suffix does not tell you whether there is a systemic component.","Necrosis is one possible cause, but the name does not establish it."],"k":"\"-itis\" signals inflammation, not its cause or duration.","rn":[["","Diseases associated with inflammation often end in what?","Diseases associated with inflammation often end in what?","-itis"]]},{"id":44007,"t":"What Is Inflammation","s":"Which pair correctly contrasts a typical cause of <b>acute</b> inflammation with a typical cause of <b>chronic</b> inflammation?","o":["Tissue necrosis from restricted blood flow; a resistant infectious agent the body cannot clear","A resistant infectious agent the body cannot clear; simple accidental trauma to the tissue","Chronic inflammatory bowel disease; exposure to chemicals or radiation","Prolonged exposure to necrotic tissue; a single bacterial infection that is cleared quickly","Autoimmune disease; simple accidental damage to cells"],"e":"Acute causes include infection, chemicals or radiation, cell or tissue injury, an excessive immune reaction and necrosis due to restricted blood flow. Chronic causes include a resistant infectious agent, prolonged exposure to endogenous or exogenous materials, some diseases such as chronic inflammatory bowel disease, and autoimmune disease. The key difference is that the agent is not removed in chronic inflammation.","w":["This pair is reversed, because a resistant agent drives chronic inflammation and trauma drives acute inflammation.","This pair is reversed, because inflammatory bowel disease is chronic and chemical exposure is an acute cause.","This pair is reversed, because prolonged exposure is chronic and a cleared infection is acute.","This pair is reversed, because autoimmune disease is listed under chronic causes."],"k":"Chronic inflammation persists because the agent is not removed.","rn":[["","What are the 5 causes of acute inflammation?","What are the 5 causes of acute inflammation?","Infection (bacterial, viral); Chemicals or radiation; Cell/tissue injury; Excessive immune reaction; Necrosis from restricted blood flow"],["","What are the 4 causes of chronic inflammation?","What are the 4 causes of chronic inflammation?","Resistant infectious agent; Prolonged exposure to materials; Some diseases (chronic IBD); Autoimmune disease"]]},{"id":44008,"t":"What Is Inflammation","s":"A patient has long-term inflammation around retained dead tissue. A second has long-term inflammation from repeated exposure to an inhaled workplace chemical. How are these agents classified?","o":["The dead tissue is endogenous and the chemical is exogenous","The dead tissue is exogenous and the chemical is endogenous","Both are endogenous, because both end up lying inside the body tissues","Both are exogenous, because neither one is a normal part of healthy functioning tissue","Neither is classified, as only pathogens count as causes of chronic inflammation"],"e":"Endogenous means from inside the body and exogenous means from outside the body. Necrotic tissue is an endogenous material, while chemicals are an exogenous one. Prolonged exposure to either can cause chronic inflammation because the agent is not removed.","w":["This reverses the two terms, since dead tissue arises from within the body.","A chemical comes from outside the body, so it is exogenous even once inhaled.","Necrotic tissue comes from the body itself, so it is endogenous.","Prolonged exposure to endogenous or exogenous materials is itself a recognised cause."],"k":"Endogenous = from inside (necrotic tissue); exogenous = from outside (chemicals).","rn":[["","Prolonged exposure causing chronic inflammation: endogenous vs exogenous examples?","Prolonged exposure causing chronic inflammation: endogenous vs exogenous examples?","Endogenous: necrotic tissue; Exogenous: chemicals"]]},{"id":44009,"t":"What Is Inflammation","s":"Name the term for disorganised cell death, which can itself act as a cause of acute inflammation when blood flow to a tissue is restricted.","a":["necrosis","tissue necrosis"],"e":"Necrosis is disorganised cell death. Tissue necrosis due to restricted blood flow is listed among the causes of acute inflammation. Prolonged exposure to necrotic tissue, an endogenous material, can also sustain chronic inflammation. Phagocytes then have to clear the debris.","k":"Necrosis (disorganised cell death) can trigger inflammation.","rn":[["","What are the 5 causes of acute inflammation?","What are the 5 causes of acute inflammation?","Infection (bacterial, viral); Chemicals or radiation; Cell/tissue injury; Excessive immune reaction; Necrosis from restricted blood flow"],["","What does inflammation follow?","What does inflammation follow?","Infection; Tissue necrosis"]]},{"id":44010,"t":"What Is Inflammation","s":"Put these events after a skin cut in the correct order: (P) phagocytes and exudate destroy bacteria; (Q) vasoactive and chemotactic factors are released; (R) phagocytes migrate by chemotaxis; (S) permeable capillaries allow an influx of exudate and cells.","o":["Q, S, R, P","S, Q, R, P","Q, R, S, P","R, Q, S, P","Q, S, P, R"],"e":"Tissue damage first releases vasoactive and chemotactic factors, which raise local blood flow and capillary permeability. Permeable capillaries then let exudate and cells into the site. Phagocytes migrate there by chemotaxis. Finally, phagocytes and the antibacterial exudate destroy bacteria.","w":["Capillaries only become permeable after the factors have been released.","Cells and fluid must be able to leave the vessel before phagocytes can complete their migration to the site.","Migration cannot come before the factors that attract the phagocytes are released.","Bacteria are destroyed by phagocytes that have already migrated to the site."],"k":"Factors → permeable capillaries and exudate → chemotaxis → killing.","rn":[["","Tissue damage releases which 2 kinds of factor? What do they trigger?","Tissue damage releases which 2 kinds of factor? What do they trigger?","Vasoactive and chemotactic factors; More local blood flow; More capillary permeability"]]},{"id":44011,"t":"What Is Inflammation","s":"After a crush injury, ruptured tissue releases a mediator that causes pain and also stimulates mast cells. What is the most direct result of this mast cell stimulation?","o":["Histamine release, with capillaries dilating and becoming permeable","Bradykinin release, with the capillaries constricting to limit further blood loss","Monocytes maturing into neutrophils inside the capillaries","Direct engulfment of bacteria by the stimulated mast cells at the wound","Fibroblast activation with immediate deposition of scar tissue"],"e":"Damaged tissue ruptures and releases bradykinin, which causes the sensation of pain and stimulates mast cells to release histamine. Capillaries then dilate and become permeable, and local temperature rises. Neutrophils migrate in and monocytes escape to mature into macrophages. Each step sets up the next.","w":["Bradykinin is the trigger released by damaged tissue, and the capillaries dilate rather than constrict.","Monocytes mature into macrophages, not neutrophils.","Engulfment is carried out by neutrophils and macrophages rather than by the mast cells here.","Scar formation comes much later in the response."],"k":"Bradykinin → pain + mast cell histamine → dilated, leaky capillaries.","rn":[["","What do damaged tissues release that causes pain? What else does it do?","What do damaged tissues release that causes pain? What else does it do?","Bradykinin; Makes mast cells release histamine"]]},{"id":44012,"t":"What Is Inflammation","s":"Macrophages that have engulfed invaders at a wound secrete protein factors. What is the main consequence for the local cell population?","o":["More neutrophils are recruited to the site","Existing neutrophils are all switched off at once","Mast cells mature into tissue macrophages at the wound","Monocytes are stopped from leaving the permeable capillaries","Plasma proteins are prevented from entering the exudate at the site"],"e":"Monocytes escape from permeable capillaries and mature into macrophages. Macrophages engulf invaders and secrete protein factors, which recruit more neutrophils. This keeps the supply of phagocytes going at the site of injury.","w":["The secreted factors recruit neutrophils rather than switching them off.","It is monocytes that mature into macrophages, not mast cells.","Monocytes are able to escape through the permeable capillaries.","Plasma proteins such as complement and C-reactive protein are part of the exudate."],"k":"Macrophages secrete factors that recruit more neutrophils.","rn":[["","Recruitment step: what happens to leukocytes?","Recruitment step: what happens to leukocytes?","Neutrophils and monocytes leave blood vessels; Migrate to site of inflammation"]]},{"id":44013,"t":"What Is Inflammation","s":"Name the fluid, containing cells and plasma proteins such as complement and C-reactive protein, that enters a site of injury through permeable capillaries.","a":["exudate","exudation","inflammatory exudate"],"e":"Permeable capillaries allow an influx of fluid, called exudate, and cells into the site of injury. The exudate carries complement and C-reactive protein. Exudation is the process of fluid, cells and plasma proteins leaking out of the vessels. Together with phagocytes, the antibacterial exudate destroys bacteria.","k":"Exudate = fluid, cells and plasma proteins leaking from vessels.","rn":[["","What does exudate contain?","What does exudate contain?","Complement; C-reactive protein"],["","What destroys bacteria at the injury site?","What destroys bacteria at the injury site?","Phagocytes; Antibacterial exudate"]]},{"id":44014,"t":"What Is Inflammation","s":"Which option correctly classifies the barriers of innate immunity?","o":["Salivary gland tissue is a physical barrier, and acidic pH is a chemical barrier","Respiratory tract epithelium is a chemical barrier, and acidic pH is a physical barrier","Anti-microbial peptides are physical barriers, and lacrimal glands are chemical barriers","Skin epithelium is a chemical barrier, and mammary gland tissue is a cellular barrier","Acidic pH is a cellular barrier, and urogenital tract epithelium is a chemical barrier"],"e":"Physical barriers are the epithelial layers of the skin, mucosal tissues (gastrointestinal, respiratory and urogenital tracts) and glandular tissues (salivary, lacrimal and mammary glands). Chemical barriers are acidic pH and anti-microbial proteins and peptides. Pathogens must breach these before meeting the cellular responses.","w":["Respiratory epithelium is physical and acidic pH is chemical, so this is reversed.","Anti-microbial peptides are chemical and lacrimal glands are physical, so this is reversed.","Skin epithelium and mammary gland tissue are both physical barriers.","Acidic pH is chemical and urogenital epithelium is physical."],"k":"Physical: epithelia and glands. Chemical: acidic pH, anti-microbial molecules.","rn":[["","What makes up chemical barriers?","What makes up chemical barriers?","Acidic pH; Antimicrobial proteins, peptides"],["","What are the 2 kinds of barrier to infection?","What are the 2 kinds of barrier to infection?","Physical; Chemical"]]},{"id":44015,"t":"What Is Inflammation","s":"Bacteria enter through a breach in the epithelium. A tissue cell detects a molecule characteristic of the bacteria. Which pairing correctly names the receptor and the thing it recognises?","o":["Pattern recognition receptor; pathogen-associated molecular pattern","Pathogen-associated molecular pattern; pattern recognition receptor","Antibody constant region receptor; the antigen-binding part of an antibody","Histamine H1 receptor; lipopolysaccharide on the pathogen's surface","Selectin; sugar groups on the surface of the bacterial wall"],"e":"After a breach, pathogens meet cells carrying cell-surface and intracellular pattern recognition receptors (PRRs). PRRs recognise pathogen-associated molecular patterns (PAMPs). Recognition activates cells to phagocytose and degrade the pathogen, and to make antimicrobial substances, cytokines and chemokines.","w":["This reverses the two: the PAMP is on the pathogen and the PRR is on the host cell.","Antibody constant region receptors bind the constant part of the antibody.","H1 receptors bind histamine and do not recognise pathogen molecules.","Selectins are endothelial adhesion molecules involved in neutrophil rolling."],"k":"PRRs on host cells recognise PAMPs on pathogens.","rn":[["","What does PAMP stand for?","What does PAMP stand for?","Pathogen-associated molecular pattern"],["","What do cells with PRRs (pattern recognition receptors) do on recognising pathogen comp…","What do cells with PRRs (pattern recognition receptors) do on recognising pathogen components?","Phagocytose and degrade the pathogen; Make antimicrobial substances; Release cytokines, chemokines"]]},{"id":44016,"t":"Immune Cells in Inflammation","s":"A cell in a tissue is infected with a virus. Which innate cell type recognises and kills such infected cells, keeping them in check?","o":["Natural killer cells","Mast cells","Fibroblasts","Plasma cells secreting antibody into the tissue","Basophils"],"e":"Natural killer (NK) cells recognise and kill some virus-infected cells. They are described as keeping things in check by detecting infected cells. This sits within the cellular responses of innate immunity, alongside phagocytosis by macrophages and dendritic cells.","w":["Mast cells release histamine and other mediators.","Fibroblasts make extracellular matrix in repair.","Plasma cells are antibody-secreting lymphocytes that increase in chronic inflammation.","Basophils are a source of histamine."],"k":"NK cells recognise and kill some virus-infected cells.","rn":[["","What does NK stand for?","What does NK stand for?","Natural killer"]]},{"id":44017,"t":"What Is Inflammation","s":"Which mechanism best explains how an innate inflammatory response helps to activate adaptive immunity?","o":["Cytokines and chemokines act together with dendritic cells that carry and present pathogens to lymphocytes","Neutrophils physically transform into antibody-secreting plasma cells once they have reached the tissue","Histamine binds lymphocytes directly and is the only signal required for T-cell responses","C-reactive protein is converted in the liver into specific antibodies during the acute phase","Fibroblasts present pathogens to T cells inside the granulation tissue at the wound"],"e":"Cytokines and chemokines, plus dendritic cells that carry and present pathogens to lymphocytes, help activate adaptive immunity, producing antibodies and T-cell responses. Innate recognition therefore feeds forward into the adaptive arm. This is why adaptive cells become prominent if a response does not resolve.","w":["Plasma cells are antibody-secreting lymphocytes and do not come from neutrophils.","Histamine's described roles are vascular, and it is not the signal for T-cell responses.","C-reactive protein is an opsonin and acute-phase protein, not a precursor of antibodies.","Fibroblasts lay down tissue in repair rather than presenting pathogens."],"k":"Dendritic cells plus cytokines and chemokines link innate to adaptive immunity.","rn":[["","How do dendritic cells link innate to adaptive immunity?","How do dendritic cells link innate to adaptive immunity?","Carry and present pathogens to lymphocytes; Activate antibody and T-cell responses"]]},{"id":44018,"t":"What Is Inflammation","s":"In which order do the five steps of acute inflammation occur?","o":["Recognition, regulation, recruitment, removal, repair","Recognition, recruitment, regulation, removal, repair","Regulation, recognition, removal, recruitment, repair","Recruitment, recognition, regulation, repair, removal","Recognition, regulation, removal, recruitment, repair"],"e":"Cells first recognise microbes or necrotic tissue. They then secrete mediators that regulate the vessels, which allows leukocytes to be recruited from blood to the site. Those leukocytes remove microbes and dead tissue. If elimination succeeds, inflammation resolves and moves on to repair.","w":["Recruitment depends on the vascular regulation that comes before it.","Nothing can be regulated until the stimulus has been recognised.","Recruitment cannot be the first step, because it needs a signal.","Removal needs recruited leukocytes, so it cannot come before recruitment."],"k":"Recognition, regulation, recruitment, removal, repair.","rn":[["","What are the 5 steps (5 Rs) of acute inflammation, in order?","What are the 5 steps (5 Rs) of acute inflammation, in order?","Recognition; Regulation; Recruitment; Removal; Repair"]]},{"id":44019,"t":"What Is Inflammation","s":"A drug blocks the secretion of mediators from macrophages that have already detected microbes. Which step of acute inflammation is <b>first</b> directly blocked?","o":["Regulation","Recognition","Repair","Removal","Recruitment"],"e":"Recognition has already happened, since the macrophages have detected microbes. Regulation is the next step, where these cells secrete mediators such as cytokines that drive inflammation and regulate the vessels. Recruitment, removal and repair would then also fail, but only because they depend on regulation.","w":["Detection has already taken place in this scenario.","Repair is the last step and would be affected only indirectly.","Removal is impaired only later, once fewer leukocytes have arrived.","Recruitment is downstream of the mediator signals, so it is not the first step blocked."],"k":"Regulation = secreting mediators that drive inflammation and regulate vessels.","rn":[["","What are the 5 steps (5 Rs) of acute inflammation, in order?","What are the 5 steps (5 Rs) of acute inflammation, in order?","Recognition; Regulation; Recruitment; Removal; Repair"],["","Regulation step: what happens to blood vessels?","Regulation step: what happens to blood vessels?","Vasodilation, via mediators; Increased vascular permeability (oedema)"]]},{"id":44020,"t":"What Is Inflammation","s":"Which <b>three</b> vessel changes make up the regulation step of acute inflammation?","o":["Vessel dilation with increased flow, vascular permeability, and leukocyte migration","Vessel constriction with reduced flow, vascular permeability, and fibroblast migration","Vessel dilation with increased flow, angiogenesis, and fibrosis of the vessel wall","Sphincter closure, reduced permeability, and leukocyte migration","Vessel dilation with reduced permeability, and platelet migration"],"e":"Secreted mediators regulate blood vessels in three ways: vessel dilation with increased blood flow, vascular permeability, and leukocyte (neutrophil or monocyte) migration. These changes bring resources to the site and let them leave the circulation. Angiogenesis and fibrosis are features of chronic inflammation and repair.","w":["Vessels dilate rather than constrict, and fibroblast migration is not part of this step.","Angiogenesis and fibrosis characterise chronic inflammation rather than this step.","Sphincters open and permeability increases in acute inflammation.","Permeability increases, and leukocytes rather than platelets are the migrating cells."],"k":"Regulation: dilation, permeability, leukocyte migration.","rn":[["","What 3 vessel and cell changes occur in acute inflammation?","What 3 vessel and cell changes occur in acute inflammation?","Vessel dilation, increased flow; Vascular permeability; Leukocyte migration"]]},{"id":44021,"t":"Wound Healing Phases","s":"Name the circulating blood cell that is the precursor of tissue macrophages at a site of inflammation.","a":["monocyte","monocytes"],"e":"Monocytes are the precursors of macrophages and circulate in the blood, while macrophages are resident in the tissues. At an inflammatory site, monocytes escape from permeable capillaries and mature into macrophages. These then engulf invaders and secrete factors that recruit more neutrophils.","k":"Monocytes circulate and mature into tissue macrophages.","rn":[["","Which cells sense a nearby injury and signal white cells?","Which cells sense a nearby injury and signal white cells?","Resident tissue cells (e.g. mast cells); Monocyte lineage"]]},{"id":44022,"t":"Inflammation and Blood Vessels","s":"In healthy resting tissue, many capillaries are empty. What changes first in acute inflammation to fill them?","o":["Arterioles dilate and precapillary sphincters open","Arterioles constrict and precapillary sphincters close","New capillaries grow into the tissue through angiogenesis","Venules collapse so that blood is forced backwards into all of the empty capillaries","Blood is diverted along the preferential channel so that flow in other capillaries stops"],"e":"In health, flow in the capillary network is intermittent because sphincters on some arterioles can stop flow to capillaries the tissue does not need. Blood mostly takes the preferential channel. In acute inflammation, arterioles initially dilate and sphincters open, so most capillaries fill. This brings resources to the site.","w":["Constriction and sphincter closure would reduce flow rather than fill the capillaries.","Angiogenesis is a feature of chronic inflammation and repair, not the initial response.","Both the arteriole and the venule dilate in acute inflammation.","Use of the preferential channel with empty capillaries describes the normal state."],"k":"Acute inflammation: arterioles dilate, sphincters open, capillaries fill.","rn":[["","What happens to blood flow in acute inflammation?","What happens to blood flow in acute inflammation?","Arterioles dilate; Sphincters open; Increased capillary network flow"]]},{"id":44023,"t":"Inflammation and Blood Vessels","s":"A researcher increases blood flow to a tissue with a drug that widens vessels, but leaves the endothelial barrier completely intact. Why would this alone <b>not</b> reproduce effective inflammation?","o":["Cells and plasma proteins must also leave the vessels, which needs increased permeability","Increased flow always makes capillaries close, so that blood no longer reaches the tissue","Widening vessels removes all leukocytes from the blood, leaving none available at the site","Inflammation does not involve any change in blood flow, so the drug has no relevant effect","Increased flow destroys plasma proteins before they can reach tissue"],"e":"Vasodilation brings resources to the site, but flow alone is not enough. Cells and plasma proteins must also get out of the vessels. Increased vascular permeability lets fluid, blood cells and plasma proteins escape to the extracellular space, a process called exudation. Both changes are therefore needed.","w":["Greater flow opens the capillary network rather than closing it.","Vasodilation does not remove leukocytes from the blood.","Increased blood flow is a central part of inflammation.","Plasma proteins are not destroyed by increased flow and they act in the exudate."],"k":"Flow delivers resources; permeability lets them out.","rn":[["","Why is increased blood flow alone not enough?","Why is increased blood flow alone not enough?","Cells and plasma proteins must also leak out; Needs vascular permeability"]]},{"id":44024,"t":"Wound Healing Phases","s":"Which option correctly links a vascular change to a local sign?","o":["Vasodilation produces warmth and redness, while permeability produces swelling","Permeability produces warmth and redness, while vasodilation produces swelling","Vasodilation produces swelling, and permeability is what causes the pain","Permeability produces pain and redness, while vasodilation produces no visible sign","Vasodilation produces pain directly, and permeability produces warmth"],"e":"Vasodilation accounts for warmth (calor) and redness (rubor). Vascular permeability lets lots of water out, which produces swelling (tumor). Pain (dolor) is linked to damage and to compounds from white cells that excite local nerves. Seeing these signs near a wound means the body is responding.","w":["This reverses the roles of the two vascular changes.","Swelling is due to permeability, and pain comes from damage and nerve-exciting compounds.","Redness is produced by vasodilation rather than permeability.","Pain is not a direct result of dilation, and warmth comes from dilation rather than permeability."],"k":"Dilation = red and hot. Permeability = swollen.","rn":[["","Why does the vascular phase cause swelling, warmth, redness?","Why does the vascular phase cause swelling, warmth, redness?","Permeability: water leaks, swelling; Vasodilation: warm and red"]]},{"id":44025,"t":"Inflammation and Blood Vessels","s":"A patient has a mild histamine-mediated reaction with leaky vessels that settles within minutes. Which mechanism best fits this short time course?","o":["Retraction of endothelial cells without damage to them","Direct burn injury to endothelial cells lasting for days","Permanent loss of the endothelial lining by necrosis","Injury to endothelium caused by a microbial toxin","New vessel formation through the process of angiogenesis"],"e":"Under histamine and other mediators, endothelial cells contract and create gaps, letting leukocytes and plasma proteins leak out. This retraction is rapid and short-lived, lasting minutes, and the cells are not damaged. Endothelial injury from burns and some microbial toxins is rapid but may last hours to days.","w":["Burn injury may be long-lived, which does not fit a reaction settling in minutes.","Necrotic loss of the lining would not settle within minutes.","Toxin-induced endothelial injury may last hours to days.","Angiogenesis characterises chronic inflammation and does not produce this quick, short-lived leak."],"k":"Endothelial retraction: rapid, minutes, no damage.","rn":[["","Why do endothelial cells contract in inflammation?","Why do endothelial cells contract in inflammation?","Create gaps; Leukocytes, plasma proteins leak out"],["","When endothelial cells contract in inflammation, are they damaged?","When endothelial cells contract in inflammation, are they damaged?","No"]]},{"id":44026,"t":"Inflammation and Blood Vessels","s":"A patient with a severe burn has vessels that keep leaking for days after the injury. How does the mechanism differ from a histamine-driven leak?","o":["The endothelium is itself damaged, so the leak may be long-lived","The endothelial cells have relaxed through nitric oxide production alone","Histamine is released continuously from fibroblasts located in the wall","The gaps are formed by actin-based contraction, which is always long-lived","Plasma proteins actively widen the gaps from the inside of the vessel"],"e":"Burns and some microbial toxins cause endothelial injury, which is rapid and may be long-lived, lasting hours to days. Neutrophils move to clear the injured area and plasma proteins flow out. Histamine-type retraction is different because the cells contract through actin without damage, and it lasts only minutes.","w":["Nitric oxide relaxes smooth muscle and does not explain a damaged endothelial lining.","Histamine comes from mast cells, basophils and platelets, not from fibroblasts.","Actin-based contraction is the short-lived mechanism lasting minutes.","Plasma proteins pass out through the gaps but do not create them."],"k":"Endothelial injury (burns, toxins): hours to days."},{"id":44027,"t":"Inflammation and Blood Vessels","s":"Endothelial cells contract under histamine to open gaps of 0.1–0.4 μm. Which cytoskeletal protein plays a role in this process?","a":["actin"],"e":"As endothelial cells contract they are not damaged, and actin plays a role in this process. The gaps in the vascular endothelium vary between 0.1 and 0.4 μm. Leukocytes and plasma proteins leak through them, and the response is rapid and short-lived.","k":"Actin mediates endothelial contraction; gaps are 0.1–0.4 μm.","rn":[["","Which protein is involved in endothelial cell contraction?","Which protein is involved in endothelial cell contraction?","Actin"]]},{"id":44028,"t":"Inflammation and Blood Vessels","s":"Which mediator is found in blood plasma rather than being released by mast cells or leucocytes, and acts on smooth muscle to cause either contraction or vasodilation?","o":["Bradykinin, a kinin","Histamine","Leukotrienes from leucocytes","Prostaglandins","Interleukin 17, a T-cell cytokine"],"e":"Kinins, such as bradykinin, come from blood plasma and act on smooth muscle to cause either contraction or vasodilation. Histamine is released from granules in mast cells and basophils. Leukotrienes are produced by mast cells and leucocytes. Knowing the source helps to tell these mediators apart.","w":["Histamine comes from granules in mast cells and basophils.","Leukotrienes are produced by mast cells and leucocytes.","Prostaglandins are produced by mast cells, macrophages and other cell types.","Interleukin 17 comes from T lymphocytes and recruits neutrophils and monocytes."],"k":"Kinins come from plasma and act on smooth muscle.","rn":[["","Kinins: example, source and effect on smooth muscle?","Kinins: example, source and effect on smooth muscle?","Bradykinin; Blood plasma; Contraction or vasodilation"]]},{"id":44029,"t":"Inflammation and Blood Vessels","s":"Which stimulus would <b>not</b> be expected to release histamine from mast cell granules?","o":["Binding of histamine to the H2 receptor on the mast cell","Tissue injury","Exposure to heat","Antibody binding to the mast cell","Exposure to cold"],"e":"Histamine is released from granules in mast cells and basophils in response to tissue injury, heat, cold or antibody binding. By contrast, mast cells and basophils carry H2 receptors that take part in negative feedback. When histamine is very abundant, this ultimately stops its secretion rather than provoking it.","w":["Tissue injury is a listed trigger of histamine release.","Heat is a listed trigger of histamine release.","Antibody binding is a listed trigger of histamine release.","Cold is a listed trigger of histamine release."],"k":"Histamine triggers: injury, heat, cold, antibody binding; H2 feedback stops release.","rn":[["","Which histamine receptor gives negative feedback? What does it do?","Which histamine receptor gives negative feedback? What does it do?","H2; Stops mast cell, basophil secretion"]]},{"id":44030,"t":"Inflammation and Blood Vessels","s":"In a blood vessel, histamine has two actions that together facilitate leukocyte recruitment. Which option describes them correctly?","o":["Smooth muscle relaxes through nitric oxide, and endothelial cells contract","Smooth muscle contracts through nitric oxide, and endothelial cells relax","Smooth muscle and endothelial cells both relax through nitric oxide production","Smooth muscle and endothelial cells both contract because of actin alone","Smooth muscle is unaffected, and endothelial cells are destroyed by the histamine"],"e":"Histamine relaxes vascular smooth muscle through the production of nitric oxide and also causes endothelial cells to contract. Relaxation widens the vessel and contraction opens gaps. Together these facilitate vasodilation and therefore leukocyte recruitment. The effect on vasodilation is mediated through H1 and H2 receptors.","w":["This reverses the two actions, since the muscle relaxes and the endothelium contracts.","Endothelial cells contract rather than relax, which is how gaps open.","Smooth muscle relaxes in vessels, so it does not contract here.","Histamine does act on smooth muscle, and retraction does not destroy endothelial cells."],"k":"Vessel histamine: muscle relaxes (NO), endothelium contracts.","rn":[["","How does histamine relax vascular smooth muscle?","How does histamine relax vascular smooth muscle?","Through nitric oxide production"],["","What are the 2 actions of histamine in vessels?","What are the 2 actions of histamine in vessels?","Relaxes smooth muscle; Contracts endothelial cells"]]},{"id":44031,"t":"Inflammation and Blood Vessels","s":"Histamine widens blood vessels by relaxing their smooth muscle, yet in the airways it makes smooth muscle contract. What is the best explanation for this difference?","o":["Histamine interacts with different receptors in different cells and tissues","Airway histamine is made from a different amino acid precursor","Vascular smooth muscle does not have any histamine receptors on its surface","Histamine is converted into prostaglandins before it reaches the smooth muscle of the airway","Airway smooth muscle responds only to the histamine that comes from platelets"],"e":"Histamine can bind any of four receptors, H1 to H4, which differ in their tissue distribution. In the airways and gastrointestinal tract it contracts smooth muscle while increasing vascular permeability and mucus secretion. In vessels it relaxes smooth muscle. This difference results from histamine interacting with one of the different receptors in cells and tissues.","w":["The precursor of histamine is histidine in all tissues.","The vasodilator effect is mediated by binding to H1 and H2 receptors.","Prostaglandins come from arachidonic acid, not from histamine.","The tissue response depends on the receptor rather than on the source cell."],"k":"Different histamine receptors give different tissue effects.","rn":[["","How many histamine receptors are there?","How many histamine receptors are there?","4 (H1 to H4); Different tissue distribution"]]},{"id":44032,"t":"Inflammation and Blood Vessels","s":"Name the amino acid precursor from which histamine is made.","a":["histidine"],"e":"Histamine's precursor is the amino acid histidine. After release from mast cells and basophils, histamine binds one of four receptors, H1 to H4. H1 is the most widespread and includes neurons, airway smooth muscle cells and blood vessels. H2 is on gastric parietal cells, smooth muscle and the heart.","k":"Histidine is the precursor of histamine.","rn":[["","What amino acid is the precursor of histamine?","What amino acid is the precursor of histamine?","Histidine"]]},{"id":44033,"t":"Inflammation and Blood Vessels","s":"A drug selectively blocks the H2 receptor on mast cells during a strong inflammatory reaction, while the H1 receptor remains free. Which effect is most predictable?","o":["Loss of the negative feedback that would stop histamine secretion when it is abundant","Complete loss of all histamine effects on neurons throughout the body","Conversion of histidine to histamine stops immediately in every mast cell","Loss of all airway smooth muscle responses that are mediated through H1 receptors","Blockade of the neutrophil rolling that is normally mediated through selectins on endothelium"],"e":"Basophils and mast cells carry H2 receptors, which take part in a negative feedback loop. This ultimately stops histamine secretion when histamine is very abundant. Blocking this receptor on mast cells would remove that brake. H1 receptors, which are widespread on neurons, airway smooth muscle and vessels, are left free in this scenario.","w":["Neuronal effects are linked to H1 receptors, which are unblocked here.","H2 blockade does not stop the cell from making histamine out of histidine.","The H1 receptor is unblocked, so its responses would remain.","Rolling depends on selectins, not on H2 receptors."],"k":"H2 on mast cells and basophils gives negative feedback on histamine secretion.","rn":[["","Which histamine receptor gives negative feedback? What does it do?","Which histamine receptor gives negative feedback? What does it do?","H2; Stops mast cell, basophil secretion"]]},{"id":44034,"t":"Inflammation and Blood Vessels","s":"Which receptor-site pairing for histamine is correct?","o":["H2 on gastric parietal cells, smooth muscle cells and the heart","H1 confined entirely to gastric parietal cells and absent elsewhere","H2 confined entirely to neurons and absent from other tissues","H1 only on mast cells and basophils, as a feedback receptor","H2 only on airway neurons, where it controls the mucus"],"e":"H1 is the more common receptor and is widespread, including neurons, airway smooth muscle cells and blood vessels. H2 is on gastric mucosa parietal cells, smooth muscle cells and the heart. H2 is also found on basophils and mast cells, where it takes part in negative feedback.","w":["H1 is widespread rather than confined to the stomach.","H2 is found on parietal cells, smooth muscle and heart, not only on neurons.","The feedback receptor on mast cells and basophils is H2.","H2 distribution is not limited to airway neurons."],"k":"H1: widespread. H2: parietal cells, smooth muscle, heart, and feedback on mast cells.","rn":[["","Where are H2 receptors found?","Where are H2 receptors found?","Gastric parietal cells; Smooth muscle; Heart"]]},{"id":44035,"t":"Inflammation and Blood Vessels","s":"A patient has cell damage that frees arachidonic acid. Which comparison of its products is correct?","o":["Prostaglandins cause vasodilation, pain and fever, while leukotrienes cause permeability and chemotaxis","Leukotrienes cause fever and pain, while prostaglandins cause leucocyte adhesion and activation","Prostaglandins are made from histidine, whereas leukotrienes are made from arachidonic acid","Both products act only through ion channels and neither binds a G-protein coupled receptor","Prostaglandins act only at the site of injury and are not involved in any systemic effect"],"e":"Arachidonic acid is a 20-carbon fatty acid available when there is cell damage, and it is the precursor of leukotrienes and prostaglandins. Prostaglandins cause vasodilation, pain and fever. Leukotrienes cause increased vascular permeability, chemotaxis, and leucocyte adhesion and activation. These metabolites, the eicosanoids, bind G-protein coupled receptors.","w":["This swaps the listed effects of the two products.","Both come from arachidonic acid, whereas histidine is the precursor of histamine.","Eicosanoids bind G-protein coupled receptors.","Fever is a systemic effect of prostaglandins."],"k":"Prostaglandins: dilation, pain, fever. Leukotrienes: permeability, chemotaxis, adhesion.","rn":[["","What are the 3 effects of prostaglandins?","What are the 3 effects of prostaglandins?","Vasodilation; Pain; Fever"],["","What are the 4 effects of leukotrienes?","What are the 4 effects of leukotrienes?","Increased vascular permeability; Chemotaxis; Leucocyte adhesion; Leucocyte activation"]]},{"id":44036,"t":"Inflammation and Blood Vessels","s":"Give the collective name for the metabolites of arachidonic acid that bind G-protein coupled receptors to mediate inflammation.","a":["eicosanoids","eicosanoid"],"e":"Eicosanoids are the metabolites of arachidonic acid, a 20-carbon fatty acid that becomes available when there is cell damage. They include prostaglandins and leukotrienes. They bind G-protein coupled receptors to mediate processes that collectively lead to inflammation.","k":"Eicosanoids = arachidonic acid metabolites.","rn":[["","What are the metabolites of arachidonic acid called?","What are the metabolites of arachidonic acid called?","Eicosanoids"],["","Which receptors do eicosanoids bind?","Which receptors do eicosanoids bind?","G protein coupled receptors"]]},{"id":44037,"t":"Wound Healing Phases","s":"Which cell listed is <b>not</b> one of the phagocytes that remove pathogens and debris at sites of inflammation?","o":["Fibroblast","Macrophage","Neutrophil","Dendritic cell","Monocyte"],"e":"Phagocytes are macrophages, monocytes, neutrophils and dendritic cells. They remove pathogens, infected cells, dead cells and cell debris. Fibroblasts are connective tissue cells that are not white cells, and they lay down tissue in the repair stage.","w":["Macrophages are phagocytes resident in the tissues.","Neutrophils engulf and destroy invading bacteria.","Dendritic cells perform phagocytosis and degradation.","Monocytes circulate in the blood and are phagocytes."],"k":"Phagocytes: macrophages, monocytes, neutrophils, dendritic cells.","rn":[["","Angiofibro (repair) phase: what happens?","Angiofibro (repair) phase: what happens?","New blood vessels form; Fibroblasts lay down tissue"],["","Repair step: how do macrophages drive repair?","Repair step: how do macrophages drive repair?","Release cytokines, growth factors; Fibroblasts lay down extracellular matrix"]]},{"id":44038,"t":"Immune Cells in Inflammation","s":"Which set contains the three classes of receptor that phagocytes use to identify targets?","o":["Pattern recognition, antibody constant region and complement factor receptors","Pattern recognition, histamine H2 and selectin receptors","Antibody constant region, insulin and arachidonic acid receptors","Complement factor, COX-2 and fibrinogen receptors","Toll-like, antigen-binding region and bradykinin receptors alone"],"e":"Phagocytes carry a variety of receptors: pattern recognition receptors, antibody constant region receptors and complement factor receptors. These identify molecules characteristic of pathogens, infected cells or dead cells. Ligand-receptor binding initiates phagocytosis and also releases molecules leading to inflammation followed by repair.","w":["Histamine H2 and selectin receptors are not the phagocyte recognition receptors listed.","Insulin and arachidonic acid receptors are not phagocyte recognition receptors.","COX-2 is an enzyme and fibrinogen is a fibrin precursor, so neither belongs here.","Toll-like receptors are one family of pattern recognition receptors, but the other two are not in the list."],"k":"Phagocyte receptors: PRRs, antibody constant region, complement factors.","rn":[["","What are the 3 types of phagocyte receptor?","What are the 3 types of phagocyte receptor?","PRRs; Antibody constant region receptors; Complement factor receptors"]]},{"id":44039,"t":"Immune Cells in Inflammation","s":"After engulfing a bacterium, a macrophage's phagosome fuses with a lysosome. Which option best describes the killing tools available to the phagocyte?","o":["Reactive oxygen species, nitric oxide and lysosomal enzymes","Histamine, bradykinin and fibrinogen","Selectins, integrins and PECAM-1 (platelet endothelial cell adhesion molecule 1)","Prostaglandins, leukotrienes and lipoxins","Interleukin 12, interleukin 17 and interferon gamma alone"],"e":"Phagocytes kill using reactive oxygen species, nitric oxide and lysosomal enzymes. The phagosome fuses with the lysosome, whose enzymes degrade the pathogen to its basic components. These same tools can leak out and damage nearby tissue, which is why inflammation can briefly make things worse.","w":["These are vascular mediators and a coagulation precursor rather than phagocyte killing tools.","These are adhesion molecules used for neutrophil recruitment.","These are lipid mediators, and lipoxins inhibit neutrophil recruitment.","These are cytokines that regulate cells rather than the direct killing tools."],"k":"Phagocytes kill with ROS, NO and lysosomal enzymes.","rn":[["","How do phagocytic receptors lead to killing of microbes?","How do phagocytic receptors lead to killing of microbes?","Microbe taken into phagosome; ROS (reactive oxygen species), lysosomal enzymes"],["","What 3 things do phagocytes use to eliminate pathogens?","What 3 things do phagocytes use to eliminate pathogens?","ROS; Nitric oxide; Lysosomal enzymes"]]},{"id":44040,"t":"Immune Cells in Inflammation","s":"A wound becomes more damaged in the first days of the inflammatory response, even though no new trauma occurred and neutrophils are abundant. What is the best explanation?","o":["Reactive oxygen species and lysosomal enzymes leak from neutrophil granules into the extracellular space","Neutrophils are making antibodies that bind directly to healthy epithelium near the wound edge","Histamine is breaking down the extracellular matrix, because it is an enzyme with proteolytic function","Fibroblasts are digesting the tissue near the wound, using enzymes taken up from the blood","Neutrophils are living for several months in the tissue and crowding out all the normal cells"],"e":"Occasionally reactive oxygen species and lysosomal enzymes leak to the extracellular space from neutrophil granules, causing nearby tissue damage. So inflammation, before making you better, can make you worse by causing some injury at the site. This is one reason why the response must be terminated. Neutrophils themselves survive only a couple of days in tissue.","w":["Neutrophils are not antibody-producing cells, as antibodies are made by plasma cells.","Histamine acts on receptors and is not described as a matrix-digesting enzyme.","Fibroblasts lay down tissue during repair.","Neutrophils survive only a couple of days after transmigration."],"k":"Leaked ROS and lysosomal enzymes cause bystander tissue damage.","rn":[["","What happens when ROS and lysosomal enzymes leak from neutrophil granules?","What happens when ROS and lysosomal enzymes leak from neutrophil granules?","Nearby tissue damage"],["","How do phagocytic receptors lead to killing of microbes?","How do phagocytic receptors lead to killing of microbes?","Microbe taken into phagosome; ROS (reactive oxygen species), lysosomal enzymes"]]},{"id":44041,"t":"Immune Cells in Inflammation","s":"A leukocyte is exposed to lipopolysaccharide. The receptor, with CD14 (cluster of differentiation 14), triggers production of mediators such as arachidonic acid metabolites and cytokines. What is the functional outcome?","o":["Amplification of the inflammatory reaction","Increased integrin avidity and chemotaxis","Termination of the inflammatory reaction","Direct killing by phagosome formation around the molecule","Fibrosis through collagen deposition at the local site"],"e":"Toll-like receptors recognise lipopolysaccharide, with CD14. The cellular response is production of mediators such as arachidonic acid metabolites and cytokines. The functional outcome is amplification of the inflammatory reaction. Integrin avidity and chemotaxis belong to G-protein coupled receptors, and microbial killing belongs to cytokine and phagocytic receptors.","w":["Integrin avidity and chemotaxis are the outcomes of G-protein coupled receptors.","Producing more mediators amplifies rather than terminates the response.","Phagosome formation is the response of phagocytic receptors.","Fibrosis is a feature of chronic inflammation and not this receptor's listed outcome."],"k":"Toll-like receptor + LPS → mediators → amplification.","rn":[["","How do TLR (Toll-like receptor) and CD14 respond to LPS?","How do TLR (Toll-like receptor) and CD14 respond to LPS?","Produce mediators (e.g. arachidonic acid metabolites, cytokines); Amplify inflammatory reaction"]]},{"id":44042,"t":"Immune Cells in Inflammation","s":"A neutrophil binds chemokines on a G-protein coupled receptor. Which sequence of outcomes follows?","o":["Cytoskeletal changes, increased integrin avidity and adhesion, then chemotaxis and migration","Lysosomal enzyme production, then direct killing with no change in cell shape or movement","Mediator production by Toll-like signalling, then amplification with no adhesion or migration","Phagosome formation around the chemokine, followed by its digestion inside a lysosome","Conversion of the neutrophil into a macrophage, then long-term residence in the tissue"],"e":"G-protein coupled receptors bind chemokines, lipid mediators and N-formyl-methionyl peptides. The cellular response is cytoskeletal changes and signal transduction. Functional outcomes are increased integrin avidity leading to adhesion to the endothelium, and chemotaxis leading to migration into tissues.","w":["Reactive oxygen species and lysosomal enzymes follow cytokine receptor signalling.","Amplification is the outcome of Toll-like receptor signalling.","Phagosome formation follows phagocytic receptor binding of a microbe.","Neutrophils do not convert into macrophages, which come from monocytes."],"k":"GPCR + chemokine → cytoskeleton change → adhesion and chemotaxis.","rn":[["","How do chemokines (GPCR, G-protein coupled receptors) signal in a leucocyte?","How do chemokines (GPCR, G-protein coupled receptors) signal in a leucocyte?","Cytoskeletal changes, signal transduction; Raise integrin avidity: adhesion to endothelium; Chemotaxis: migration into tissues"]]},{"id":44043,"t":"Immune Cells in Inflammation","s":"Interferon gamma binds to its receptor on a leukocyte. What is the direct cellular response and overall outcome?","o":["Production of reactive oxygen species and lysosomal enzymes, giving killing of microbes","Cytoskeletal changes, giving rolling on selectins","Production of arachidonic acid metabolites, giving amplification of inflammation","Phagosome formation, giving complement activation throughout the plasma","Release of histamine granules, giving vasodilation"],"e":"Cytokine receptors bind cytokines such as interferon gamma. The cellular response is production of reactive oxygen species and lysosomal enzymes. The functional outcome is microbicidal activity, the killing of microbes. Phagocytic receptors reach the same outcome by a different route, through phagosome formation around a microbe.","w":["Cytoskeletal changes are the response of G-protein coupled receptors.","Producing arachidonic acid metabolites follows Toll-like receptor binding.","Phagosome formation is the response of phagocytic receptors and does not activate complement here.","Histamine granule release is a function of mast cells and basophils."],"k":"Cytokine receptor (IFN-γ) → ROS and enzymes → microbial killing.","rn":[["","How do phagocytic receptors lead to killing of microbes?","How do phagocytic receptors lead to killing of microbes?","Microbe taken into phagosome; ROS (reactive oxygen species), lysosomal enzymes"]]},{"id":44044,"t":"Immune Cells in Inflammation","s":"Which receptor group contains mannose, scavenger and opsonin receptors?","o":["Phagocytic receptors","Toll-like receptors","Cytokine receptors","G-protein coupled receptors that bind chemokines","Selectin receptors on the neutrophil"],"e":"Phagocytic receptors include mannose receptors, scavenger receptors and opsonin receptors. For example, a mannose receptor recognises the sugar mannose on a microbe and triggers signalling that changes cell activity and ultimately kills the microbe. The microbe is taken into a phagosome.","w":["Toll-like receptors recognise PAMPs such as lipopolysaccharide.","Cytokine receptors bind cytokines such as interferon gamma.","Chemokine G-protein coupled receptors drive cytoskeletal changes rather than engulfment.","Neutrophil receptors that detect selectins mediate rolling."],"k":"Phagocytic receptors: mannose, scavenger, opsonin.","rn":[["","What are the 3 phagocytic receptors?","What are the 3 phagocytic receptors?","Mannose receptors; Scavenger receptors; Opsonin receptors"]]},{"id":44045,"t":"Immune Cells in Inflammation","s":"Name the family of pattern recognition receptors that recognises PAMPs such as lipopolysaccharide on macrophages.","a":["toll-like receptors","toll like receptors","toll-like receptor","toll like receptor","tlr","tlrs"],"e":"Toll-like receptors (TLRs) are a family of pattern recognition receptors that recognise PAMPs on pathogens, and they are described as macrophage specific. A key ligand is lipopolysaccharide, with CD14. Binding produces mediators and so amplifies the inflammatory reaction.","k":"TLRs are PRRs recognising PAMPs such as LPS.","rn":[["","How do TLR (Toll-like receptor) and CD14 respond to LPS?","How do TLR (Toll-like receptor) and CD14 respond to LPS?","Produce mediators (e.g. arachidonic acid metabolites, cytokines); Amplify inflammatory reaction"],["","What does TLR stand for?","What does TLR stand for?","Toll-like Receptor"]]},{"id":44046,"t":"Immune Cells in Inflammation","s":"A patient lacks functional selectins on the endothelium. Which step of neutrophil recruitment fails <b>first</b>?","o":["Rolling along the vessel wall","Firm stopping through integrins","Crossing through PECAM-1 (platelet endothelial cell adhesion molecule 1)","Killing of microbes in the tissue","Slowing of blood flow through vessel widening"],"e":"Vessel widening slows blood flow, and neutrophils accumulate near the wall. This stage does not need selectins. Endothelial cells then express P-selectin or E-selectin, which neutrophil receptors detect, giving a loose low-affinity bond that produces rolling. Without selectins, rolling fails first, and later steps are lost because they follow it.","w":["Integrin binding is lost only because the rolling step before it has failed.","Crossing comes after firm adhesion and is not the first step lost.","Killing is a much later step that depends on arrival at the site.","Stasis is due to vessel widening and does not require selectins."],"k":"Selectins = rolling; first adhesive step in recruitment.","rn":[["","Why do neutrophils roll along the endothelium?","Why do neutrophils roll along the endothelium?","Selectin adhesion is loose; Low affinity"]]},{"id":44047,"t":"Immune Cells in Inflammation","s":"Neutrophils in a patient roll along the endothelium normally but fail to come to a stop, and few reach the tissue. Which molecule class is most likely defective?","o":["Integrins","Selectins","Prostaglandins","Eicosanoids","Opsonins"],"e":"Rolling is mediated by loose selectin bonds, and it is intact here. The firm stop requires integrin ligands binding strongly to neutrophil integrins, which makes the connection stable. A failure to stop therefore points to integrins. Chemokines activate integrins from low-affinity to high-affinity states.","w":["Selectin-mediated rolling is normal in this patient.","Prostaglandins mediate vasodilation, pain and fever rather than the firm stop.","Eicosanoids are arachidonic acid metabolites and are not the adhesion molecules.","Opsonins tag pathogens for phagocytes."],"k":"Integrins = firm stop.","rn":[["","What stops the rolling of neutrophils?","What stops the rolling of neutrophils?","Integrin ligands binding neutrophil integrins; Strong binding"]]},{"id":44048,"t":"Systemic Inflammation","s":"Neutrophils in a tissue sample are found firmly stuck to the inside of the venule wall in great numbers, but they are not crossing into the tissue. Which molecule most likely fails to act?","o":["PECAM-1 (platelet endothelial cell adhesion molecule 1)","P-selectin","E-selectin","Sialyl-Lewis X-modified glycoprotein","Lipoxin"],"e":"Firm adhesion means selectin and integrin steps have worked. Transmigration through the gaps follows an interaction with PECAM-1, also known as CD31, which has been likened to a passport check. Failure at this final stage leaves neutrophils stuck inside the vessel.","w":["P-selectin acts in rolling, which has already happened.","E-selectin acts in rolling, which has already happened.","This molecule on the neutrophil is involved in rolling, which came earlier.","Lipoxins inhibit neutrophil recruitment, so their absence would not stop crossing."],"k":"PECAM-1 (CD31) is needed for transmigration.","rn":[["","What do TNF and IL-1 do to local endothelial cells?","What do TNF and IL-1 do to local endothelial cells?","Increase adhesion molecule expression"],["","What molecule acts as a \"passport check\" before transmigration? Other name?","What molecule acts as a \"passport check\" before transmigration? Other name?","PECAM-1; CD31"],["","Neutrophil recruitment: job of selectins vs integrins vs PECAM-1?","Neutrophil recruitment: job of selectins vs integrins vs PECAM-1?","Selectins: rolling; Integrins: stable adhesion; PECAM-1: transmigration"]]},{"id":44049,"t":"Immune Cells in Inflammation","s":"Why does a neutrophil <b>roll</b> rather than stop when it first binds a selectin?","o":["The selectin bond is low affinity, so the cell lets go and binds the next one","The selectin bond is high affinity but is cleaved at once by nitric oxide","Selectins are only found on the neutrophil, so the wall has nothing to bind to","Selectins contract the endothelial cells, pushing the neutrophil further along","Blood flow speeds up on binding, so the attachment is torn apart"],"e":"The selectin bond is loose or low affinity, so the neutrophil lets go and rolls along the endothelium from one selectin to the next. Integrin ligands then bind strongly to neutrophil integrins, giving a more stable connection and stopping the rolling. Blood flow is actually slowed by vessel widening, which helps neutrophils reach the wall.","w":["The selectin bond is low affinity, and nitric oxide acts on smooth muscle and in killing.","P-selectin and E-selectin are expressed by endothelial cells.","Histamine and other mediators contract endothelial cells, not selectins.","Vessel widening slows blood flow rather than speeding it up."],"k":"Rolling is due to low-affinity selectin bonds.","rn":[["","Why do neutrophils roll along the endothelium?","Why do neutrophils roll along the endothelium?","Selectin adhesion is loose; Low affinity"]]},{"id":44050,"t":"Immune Cells in Inflammation","s":"Which signal most directly causes selectin and integrin expression needed for neutrophil adhesion in an infected tissue?","o":["Tumour necrosis factor and interleukin 1 from macrophages that have captured microbes","Complement-derived membrane attack complexes on the endothelial cell surface","Arachidonic acid released from the neutrophil's own nucleus after degranulation","Fibrinogen from the exudate binding to the wall of the arteriole upstream","Lipoxins made in parallel with pro-inflammatory molecules"],"e":"Macrophages that have captured microbes or necrotic tissue release cytokines such as tumour necrosis factor and interleukin 1. Cytokines and chemokines from damaged or infected tissue induce selectin and integrin expression. Tumour necrosis factor stimulates expression of endothelial adhesion molecules. Lipoxins do the opposite by inhibiting adhesion.","w":["The membrane attack complex damages microbial membranes and is not the adhesion signal.","Arachidonic acid metabolites do not act as the listed trigger for selectin expression.","Fibrinogen forms a fibrin network that traps microbes and aids healing.","Lipoxins inhibit neutrophil chemotaxis and adhesion."],"k":"Macrophage TNF and IL-1 induce endothelial adhesion molecules.","rn":[["","Which cells release the cytokines (TNF, IL-1) that switch on selectins?","Which cells release the cytokines (TNF, IL-1) that switch on selectins?","Macrophages that captured microbes or necrotic tissue"]]},{"id":44051,"t":"Immune Cells in Inflammation","s":"What is the correct name for the process in which leukocytes pass through capillary walls into tissue?","a":["extravasation","transmigration","diapedesis"],"e":"Extravasation is the passage of cells through capillaries to tissue. Gaps and leakiness alone are not enough, as leukocytes must be actively recruited. The steps are slowing, signalling, selectin-mediated rolling, integrin-mediated firm stopping and transmigration via PECAM-1. They then travel to the site.","k":"Extravasation = cells passing through capillaries to tissue.","rn":[["","What is extravasation?","What is extravasation?","Cells leave capillaries for tissue"]]},{"id":44052,"t":"Immune Cells in Inflammation","s":"What is the immediate role of stasis in leukocyte recruitment?","o":["Slowed flow lets neutrophils accumulate near the endothelium","Slowed flow stops the release of all chemokines from the macrophages","Slowed flow triggers transmigration through PECAM-1 with no need for any adhesion steps","Slowed flow prevents plasma proteins from escaping the vessel","Slowed flow converts monocytes into neutrophils inside the vessel"],"e":"The increase in vessel diameter slows blood flow, which is stasis. Neutrophils therefore accumulate near the endothelium, where they can interact with adhesion molecules. Rolling, firm adhesion and transmigration then follow in sequence. Stasis itself does not replace those steps.","w":["Chemokine release from macrophages is not stopped by slow flow.","Transmigration still requires the prior adhesion steps.","Plasma proteins escape through increased permeability despite the slower flow.","Monocytes mature into macrophages, not neutrophils."],"k":"Stasis brings neutrophils near the vessel wall.","rn":[["","Why do neutrophils accumulate near the vessel wall?","Why do neutrophils accumulate near the vessel wall?","Wider vessel slows blood flow; Called stasis"]]},{"id":44053,"t":"Immune Cells in Inflammation","s":"A biopsy taken one day after an acute skin injury is plotted on a graph of activity against days 1 to 3. In which order do the curves rise?","o":["Oedema, then neutrophils, then monocytes and macrophages","Neutrophils, then monocytes and macrophages, then oedema","Monocytes and macrophages, then oedema, then neutrophils","Oedema, then monocytes and macrophages, then neutrophils","Lymphocytes, then plasma cells, then oedema"],"e":"The acute time course has oedema first, then neutrophils, then monocytes and macrophages, over days 1 to 3. This fits the sequence of vascular changes followed by recruitment of phagocytes. Under the microscope, acute inflammation is seen as accumulations of phagocytes, particularly neutrophils.","w":["Oedema comes from vascular change and rises before the cells.","Monocytes and macrophages come last in this acute sequence.","Neutrophils rise before monocytes and macrophages.","Lymphocytes and plasma cells characterise chronic inflammation."],"k":"Acute time course: oedema → neutrophils → monocytes/macrophages.","rn":[["","What are the 4 phagocytes?","What are the 4 phagocytes?","Macrophages; Monocytes; Neutrophils; Dendritic cells"]]},{"id":44054,"t":"Immune Cells in Inflammation","s":"A pathologist looks at a tissue section packed with neutrophils and monocytes, and no lymphocytes or plasma cells are prominent. Which is the most likely interpretation?","o":["Acute inflammation, seen as accumulations of phagocytes","Chronic inflammation, because the phagocytes are present in large numbers","Granulomatous inflammation, because any tissue monocytes are by definition epithelioid cells","Resolved inflammation, because phagocytes appear only once the injury has cleared","Fibrosis, because neutrophils are the source of scar tissue"],"e":"Acute inflammation shows accumulations of neutrophils and of monocytes, which ultimately differentiate into macrophages. So it is seen as accumulations of phagocytes. Chronic inflammation is marked by lymphocytes, plasma cells and macrophages, while a granuloma has macrophages and lymphocytes around a central area.","w":["Chronic inflammation is marked by lymphocytes, plasma cells and macrophages.","Epithelioid cells are activated macrophages rather than ordinary tissue monocytes.","In complete resolution the busy environment has quietened and been cleared.","Fibrosis is laid down in repair and is not produced by neutrophils."],"k":"Acute inflammation on histology = accumulations of phagocytes.","rn":[["","Image card: What type of inflammation does this histology show? Which cell has accumulated?","What type of inflammation does this histology show? Which cell has accumulated?","Acute inflammation; Neutrophils"],["","What causes tumor in inflammation?","What causes tumor in inflammation?","Fluid accumulation at injury site"],["","Why may abscess formation delay resolution?","Why may abscess formation delay resolution?","Neutrophil accumulation; Considered chronic inflammation"]]},{"id":44055,"t":"Mediators of Inflammation","s":"Which mediator-source pairing is correct?","o":["Complement factors and opsonins: plasma proteins produced in the liver","Histamine: plasma protein produced by the liver during the acute phase","Prostaglandins: made by the liver from histidine and released into plasma","Chemokines: made only by hepatocytes and stored in the plasma until used","Interleukin 1: made only by fibroblasts in the granulation tissue"],"e":"Complement factors and opsonins such as C-reactive protein are plasma proteins made in the liver. These are the exceptions, since most mediators come from tissue-resident or blood-circulating leukocytes. Histamine comes from mast cells, basophils and platelets. Prostaglandins come from mast cells and leucocytes.","w":["Histamine comes from mast cells, basophils and platelets.","Prostaglandins come from mast cells and leucocytes, and from arachidonic acid.","Chemokines come from leucocytes and activated macrophages, among other cells.","Interleukin 1 comes from macrophages, endothelial cells and some epithelial cells."],"k":"Complement and opsonins come from the liver; most other mediators from leukocytes.","rn":[["","Which mediators are made by leukocytes, and which by the liver?","Which mediators are made by leukocytes, and which by the liver?","Leukocytes: histamine, cytokines, chemokines; Liver: complement, opsonins"]]},{"id":44056,"t":"Inflammation and Blood Vessels","s":"A severely ill patient has hypotension (shock) from an inflammatory process and also has endothelial activation at the local site. Which mediator group best explains both effects?","o":["Tumour necrosis factor, interleukin 1 and interleukin 6","Histamine released only from platelets","Opsonins such as C-reactive protein","Lipoxins","Chemokines acting as cell attractants only"],"e":"The cytokines tumour necrosis factor, interleukin 1 and interleukin 6 come from macrophages, endothelial cells and mast cells. Locally they cause endothelial activation, meaning expression of adhesion molecules. Systemically they cause fever and hypotension (shock). This dual local and systemic action is not shared by the other mediators listed.","w":["Histamine causes vasodilation and permeability, but this does not include endothelial adhesion activation.","Opsonins induce phagocytosis.","Lipoxins inhibit neutrophil recruitment.","Chemokines cause chemotaxis and leukocyte activation."],"k":"TNF, IL-1, IL-6: local endothelial activation; systemic fever and shock.","rn":[["","What does TNF stand for?","What does TNF stand for?","Tumor Necrosis Factor"]]},{"id":44057,"t":"What Is Inflammation","s":"Which function is <b>not</b> attributed to complement factors in the inflammatory response?","o":["Stimulating fibroblasts to lay down collagen scar","Leukocyte chemotaxis","Pathogen killing via a membrane attack complex","Vasodilation through mast cell stimulation","Tagging pathogens through opsonisation"],"e":"Complement factors are involved in leukocyte chemotaxis and inflammation, pathogen killing through the membrane attack complex, and vasodilation through mast cell stimulation. C3b also opsonises pathogens. Fibroblast activation is due to cytokines and growth factors in the repair step, not complement.","w":["Leukocyte chemotaxis is a listed complement action.","The membrane attack complex is a listed complement action.","Mast cell stimulation leading to vasodilation is a listed complement action.","Opsonisation by C3b is a listed complement action."],"k":"Complement: chemotaxis, MAC killing, vasodilation, opsonisation.","rn":[["","Repair step: how do macrophages drive repair?","Repair step: how do macrophages drive repair?","Release cytokines, growth factors; Fibroblasts lay down extracellular matrix"],["","Angiofibro (repair) phase: what happens?","Angiofibro (repair) phase: what happens?","New blood vessels form; Fibroblasts lay down tissue"]]},{"id":44058,"t":"Mediators of Inflammation","s":"Which statement about C-reactive protein is correct?","o":["It is a pentameric liver protein induced mainly by interleukin 6 acting on its gene","It is a dimeric neutrophil product induced mainly by histamine acting on its gene","It is a mast cell granule protein, released on heat, cold or antibody binding only","It is purely anti-inflammatory and has no ability to activate the complement system","It is a marker that rises slowly over months and remains fixed after the stimulus is removed"],"e":"C-reactive protein is a pentameric protein made by the liver. It is an acute-phase protein induced primarily by interleukin 6 acting on the gene responsible for its transcription. It has both proinflammatory and anti-inflammatory properties and can activate the classic complement pathway. Levels rise and fall rapidly with the stimulus.","w":["It is made by the liver, and interleukin 6 rather than histamine is the main inducer.","Those release triggers apply to histamine from mast cells.","It has proinflammatory as well as anti-inflammatory properties, and it can activate complement.","Levels rise and fall rapidly with the onset and removal of the stimulus."],"k":"CRP: pentameric, liver, IL-6-induced, rises and falls fast.","rn":[["","What makes CRP (C-reactive protein) and in what form?","What makes CRP (C-reactive protein) and in what form?","Liver; Pentameric (5-part) acute-phase protein"]]},{"id":44059,"t":"Mediators of Inflammation","s":"A patient's C-reactive protein falls quickly once an infected wound has been cleared. A different patient has a persistently raised level for months. What is the most appropriate interpretation?","o":["The first fits removal of the stimulus; the second can be seen in chronic inflammation","The first proves that the liver has failed; the second proves that sepsis has resolved","Both results prove a bacterial cause, because only bacteria raise C-reactive protein","Both results are uninterpretable, as levels do not change with inflammation at all","The first shows autoimmune disease; the second shows the acute phase has only just begun"],"e":"C-reactive protein is abundant in plasma during inflammation, so raised blood levels indicate inflammation. Levels rise and fall rapidly with the onset and removal of the stimulus, and persistently elevated levels can be seen in chronic inflammatory conditions. There are numerous causes, acute and chronic, infectious or non-infectious. It therefore does not by itself prove the cause.","w":["A rapid fall fits removal of the stimulus and does not indicate liver failure.","Causes can be non-infectious as well as infectious.","Levels do change with inflammation, which is why the test is useful.","A rapid fall does not show autoimmune disease, and months of elevation is not the start of an acute phase."],"k":"CRP tracks inflammation; persistent elevation in chronic conditions; many causes.","rn":[["","How do CRP levels change with an inflammatory stimulus?","How do CRP levels change with an inflammatory stimulus?","Rise and fall rapidly; Persistently high in chronic inflammation"]]},{"id":44060,"t":"Mediators of Inflammation","s":"Which mechanism explains how C-reactive protein speeds the removal of debris and pathogens?","o":["It binds targets such as phosphocholine and activates complement and phagocytes via Fc receptors","It is an enzyme that directly digests bacterial walls and removes debris from inside the phagosome","It binds selectins on the endothelium and so makes the neutrophils roll much more rapidly","It inhibits interleukin 6 production by the liver and so limits the size of the acute phase","It closes capillary sphincters, keeping phagocytes concentrated at the site of the injury"],"e":"C-reactive protein recognises and helps clear foreign pathogens and damaged cells by binding phosphocholine, phospholipids, histone, chromatin and fibronectin. It can activate the classic complement pathway and activate phagocytic cells via Fc (fragment crystallisable) receptors. This speeds removal of debris, damaged or apoptotic cells and pathogens. It can worsen tissue damage in certain cases.","w":["It works as a tag and activator, not as a digesting enzyme in the phagosome.","Selectin-mediated rolling does not involve C-reactive protein.","Interleukin 6 induces C-reactive protein, and this option describes the reverse.","Capillary sphincters open in acute inflammation, and this is not an action of C-reactive protein."],"k":"CRP binds targets, activates complement and Fc-receptor phagocytes.","rn":[["","How does CRP help clear pathogens?","How does CRP help clear pathogens?","Binds phosphocholine, phospholipids, histone, chromatin, fibronectin; Activates classical complement pathway; Activates phagocytes via Fc receptors"]]},{"id":44061,"t":"Mediators of Inflammation","s":"Antigen-antibody complexes, particularly involving immunoglobulin M, form on a pathogen and start a plasma protein cascade. Which pathway has started, and which outcome is it paired with?","o":["Classical pathway; inflammatory cell recruitment","Lectin pathway; pathogen opsonisation","Alternative pathway; pathogen elimination","Classical pathway; pathogen opsonisation by lectins","Lectin pathway; inflammatory cell recruitment"],"e":"The classical pathway is triggered by antigen-antibody complexes, and immunoglobulin M is very good at engaging complement. Its paired outcome is inflammatory cell recruitment. The lectin pathway is triggered by PAMP recognition by lectins and is paired with opsonisation. The alternative pathway starts by spontaneous hydrolysis on pathogenic surfaces and is paired with pathogen elimination.","w":["The lectin pathway is triggered by lectins recognising PAMPs.","The alternative pathway starts by spontaneous hydrolysis on pathogenic surfaces.","Opsonisation is paired with the lectin pathway, and lectins are not the classical trigger.","Antigen-antibody complexes trigger the classical pathway, not the lectin pathway."],"k":"Classical: antigen-antibody complexes → inflammatory cell recruitment.","rn":[["","What does each complement pathway lead to?","What does each complement pathway lead to?","Classical: inflammatory cell recruitment; Lectin: pathogen opsonisation; Alternative: pathogen elimination"]]},{"id":44062,"t":"Mediators of Inflammation","s":"A pathogen surface triggers complement without any antibody and without lectin binding. Which trigger is operating?","o":["Spontaneous hydrolysis on the pathogenic surface","Antigen-antibody complexes formed with immunoglobulin M","Recognition of pathogen-associated molecular patterns by lectins","Direct stimulation of the cascade by neutrophil selectins","Histamine binding to complement factors within the plasma"],"e":"The alternative pathway is triggered by spontaneous hydrolysis on pathogenic surfaces, with no need for antibody or lectin. All three pathways feed into initiation and then amplification. Its paired outcome is pathogen elimination.","w":["Antigen-antibody complexes trigger the classical pathway.","Lectin recognition of PAMPs triggers the lectin pathway.","Selectins are endothelial adhesion molecules and do not start complement.","Histamine can be released as a result of complement but does not trigger the cascade."],"k":"Alternative pathway: spontaneous hydrolysis on pathogen surfaces."},{"id":44063,"t":"Mediators of Inflammation","s":"A patient's bacteria are coated with a complement fragment that makes them easily identifiable to phagocytes. Which fragment is this?","o":["C3b","C3a","C5a","C5b","C-reactive protein"],"e":"C3 is cleaved to C3a and C3b. C3b is an opsonising molecule deposited on pathogens, making them easily identifiable to phagocytes, and it also participates in cleaving C5. C3a and C5a are anaphylatoxins, while C5b is a component of the membrane attack complex.","w":["C3a is an anaphylatoxin that promotes inflammation.","C5a is an anaphylatoxin that promotes inflammation.","C5b contributes to the membrane attack complex.","C-reactive protein is an opsonin, but it is not a complement fragment."],"k":"C3b = opsonin and helps cleave C5.","rn":[["","What is the job of C3b?","What is the job of C3b?","Cleaves C5 to C5a, C5b; Opsonises pathogens"],["","What is the most abundant complement factor? Cleaved to what?","What is the most abundant complement factor? Cleaved to what?","C3; C3a and C3b"]]},{"id":44064,"t":"Mediators of Inflammation","s":"A bacterium is destroyed by damage to its membrane, caused by a complement-derived structure. Which fragment is a protein component of that structure?","o":["C5b","C3a","C3b","C5a","Fibrinogen"],"e":"C5b is a protein component of the membrane attack complex, which destroys pathogens by damaging their membrane. It arises when C3b participates in the cleavage of C5 into C5a and C5b. C3a and C5a promote inflammation, and C3b tags pathogens.","w":["C3a is an anaphylatoxin that promotes inflammation.","C3b is an opsonising molecule and does not form this structure.","C5a is an anaphylatoxin that promotes inflammation.","Fibrinogen forms a fibrin network that traps microbes."],"k":"C5b contributes to the membrane attack complex.","rn":[["","C5b is a component of what? How does that kill pathogens?","C5b is a component of what? How does that kill pathogens?","Membrane attack complex; Damages their membrane"]]},{"id":44065,"t":"Mediators of Inflammation","s":"Which option best states what anaphylatoxins do, and which complement fragments they are?","o":["C3a and C5a; they trigger cytokine release, leucocyte recruitment, degranulation and histamine or prostaglandin release","C3b and C5b; they coat pathogens for phagocytes and create pores in microbial membranes","C3a and C3b; they are inhibitors that terminate the cascade when it has become excessive","C5a and C5b; they form the membrane attack complex together","C3b and C5a; they are the two plasma proteins that induce fever directly"],"e":"C3a and C5a are anaphylatoxins. They promote inflammation by triggering release of pro-inflammatory cytokines, leucocyte recruitment, granulocyte degranulation and histamine or prostaglandin release. C3b is the opsonin and C5b is part of the membrane attack complex.","w":["These fragments are the opsonin and the membrane attack complex component, not anaphylatoxins.","These fragments promote or tag rather than terminate.","C5a is an anaphylatoxin and is not part of the membrane attack complex.","C3b is an opsonin, and fever is due to cytokines and prostaglandins."],"k":"Anaphylatoxins = C3a and C5a: promote inflammation.","rn":[["","What do C3a and C5a trigger?","What do C3a and C5a trigger?","Release of pro-inflammatory cytokines; Leucocyte recruitment, degranulation; Histamine, prostaglandin release"]]},{"id":44066,"t":"Mediators of Inflammation","s":"Name the most abundant complement factor, which is cleaved into two fragments including an opsonin.","a":["c3","complement c3","c3 complement"],"e":"Complement factors circulate in plasma in their inactive form. The most abundant, C3, is cleaved to C3a and C3b. C3b participates in the cleavage of C5 to C5a and C5b. This gives signal amplification involving other complement factors, which is why it is a true cascade.","k":"C3 is the most abundant complement factor.","rn":[["","What is the most abundant complement factor? Cleaved to what?","What is the most abundant complement factor? Cleaved to what?","C3; C3a and C3b"]]},{"id":44067,"t":"Mediators of Inflammation","s":"A genetic defect prevents cleavage of C3. Which consequence is most logical?","o":["Both opsonisation by C3b and downstream C5 cleavage are impaired","Only histamine synthesis from histidine is impaired in the mast cells","Only classical pathway triggering is lost; the downstream cascade is unaffected","Plasma complement factors become permanently active and no longer circulate inactive","Only C5b production is reduced, while pathogens stay fully tagged by C3b"],"e":"C3 is cleaved to C3a and C3b, and C3b participates in the cleavage of C5. So a failure of C3 cleavage removes the opsonin C3b and the anaphylatoxin C3a. It also impairs generation of C5a and C5b, which reduces membrane attack complex formation. This shows how each step acts on the next in a cascade.","w":["Histamine synthesis from histidine does not depend on C3 cleavage.","All pathways feed into shared initiation and amplification, so the effects extend beyond one trigger.","The factors would fail to activate rather than become permanently active.","C3b could not be generated if C3 could not be cleaved."],"k":"C3 cleavage is upstream of opsonisation and C5 cleavage.","rn":[["","What is the job of C3b?","What is the job of C3b?","Cleaves C5 to C5a, C5b; Opsonises pathogens"]]},{"id":44068,"t":"Mediators of Inflammation","s":"Which mechanism does <b>not</b> contribute to ending an acute inflammatory response?","o":["Persistent expression of mediators after the stimulus has gone","Mediators are only expressed in response to a stimulus","Mediators have short half-lives and decay quickly","Neutrophils survive only a couple of days in tissue","Lipoxins are made in parallel with pro-inflammatory molecules"],"e":"The built-in off switches are that mediators need a trigger, mediators decay quickly, neutrophils are short-lived after transmigration, and anti-inflammatory molecules such as lipoxins are made in parallel. Persistent mediator expression would prolong the response. A prolonged response is one of the settings in which leukocytes cause harm.","w":["Stimulus dependence is a built-in off switch.","Short mediator half-lives are a built-in off switch.","Short neutrophil survival is a built-in off switch.","Lipoxins are anti-inflammatory and limit the response."],"k":"Off switches: stimulus-dependent, short half-lives, short-lived neutrophils, lipoxins.","rn":[["","What are the 4 ways inflammation is terminated?","What are the 4 ways inflammation is terminated?","Mediators only made with stimulus; Mediators have short half-lives; Neutrophils have short half-lives; Anti-inflammatory molecules"]]},{"id":44069,"t":"Mediators of Inflammation","s":"Name the anti-inflammatory molecules, made in parallel with pro-inflammatory molecules, that inhibit neutrophil chemotaxis and adhesion to vascular endothelium.","a":["lipoxins","lipoxin"],"e":"Anti-inflammatory molecules such as lipoxins are made in parallel with pro-inflammatory molecules. Lipoxins inhibit neutrophil recruitment by inhibiting chemotaxis and adhesion to the vascular endothelium. Some cytokines are also anti-inflammatory. This helps to limit the damage caused by leaking phagocyte contents and neutrophil build-up.","k":"Lipoxins inhibit neutrophil recruitment.","rn":[["","Lipoxins: what type of molecule? What do they inhibit?","Lipoxins: what type of molecule? What do they inhibit?","Anti-inflammatory; Neutrophil recruitment (chemotaxis, adhesion)"]]},{"id":44070,"t":"Mediators of Inflammation","s":"Why is it important that an inflammatory response is terminated, rather than simply being allowed to continue in case further microbes appear?","o":["Leaking phagocyte contents and neutrophil build-up damage host tissue","Mediators eventually become antigens that activate lymphocytes against microbes","Neutrophils live for years in the tissue and block all later wound healing","Vasodilation permanently reverses once the mediators have run out in the tissue","Complement factors stop being produced by the liver if used for a long period"],"e":"Inflammation must stop because leaking phagocyte contents and neutrophil build-up cause damage. Leukocytes cause harm when the response is prolonged, when they attack host tissues as in autoimmune disease, and with unchecked release of enzyme-rich granules. The response is therefore kept to a minimum. Neutrophils themselves live only for a couple of days in the tissue.","w":["This is not given as the reason for terminating the response.","Neutrophils survive only a couple of days after transmigration.","Vasodilation is a controlled response, and its reversal is not the harm being avoided.","Depletion of liver production is not the stated reason for termination."],"k":"Prolonged inflammation harms host tissue, so it must end.","rn":[["","What are the 3 ways leukocytes can damage tissue?","What are the 3 ways leukocytes can damage tissue?","Prolonged inflammatory response; Attacking host tissues (autoimmune); Unchecked granule enzyme release"],["","What happens when ROS and lysosomal enzymes leak from neutrophil granules?","What happens when ROS and lysosomal enzymes leak from neutrophil granules?","Nearby tissue damage"]]},{"id":44071,"t":"Acute to Chronic Inflammation","s":"A patient with a local infection responds well to intravenous antibiotics. Which feature of acute inflammation most directly helps the drug to reach the site?","o":["Increased vascular permeability","Fibrin deposition around the microbes","Neutrophil short half-life","Dilution of toxins by the exudate","Fever acting on the hypothalamus"],"e":"Increased vascular permeability assists transport of blood-circulating antibodies and drugs, such as antibiotics, to the site of infection. Exudation also dilutes toxins, and fibrinogen in the exudate forms a network that traps microbes. These are separate local benefits, but only permeability describes transport of the drug.","w":["Fibrin traps microbes rather than delivering the drug.","Short neutrophil survival helps terminate the response.","Dilution lowers toxin concentration and is not a route for drug delivery.","Fever is a systemic effect and does not describe drug delivery."],"k":"Permeability helps antibodies and drugs reach the site.","rn":[["","What does increased vascular permeability help transport to the infection?","What does increased vascular permeability help transport to the infection?","Blood circulating antibodies; Drugs (e.g. antibiotics)"]]},{"id":44072,"t":"Acute to Chronic Inflammation","s":"A pathogen secretes a toxin at a wound. How does the fluid released during inflammation help the patient?","o":["It dilutes the toxin concentration, diminishing its effects","It chemically converts the toxin to arachidonic acid","It carries the toxin away through fibrin into nearby lymph nodes for storage","It raises the toxin's affinity for phagocyte receptors","It neutralises the toxin by opening precapillary sphincters"],"e":"Exudation and fluid release dilute the concentration of toxins produced by pathogens and so diminish their effects. This is one of three local benefits, along with delivery of antibodies and drugs and a fibrin network that can trap microbes. The other options are not mechanisms in this response.","w":["Arachidonic acid comes from damaged cells, not from toxins.","Fibrin forms a trapping network and is not described as moving toxin to storage.","Phagocyte receptor affinity for the toxin is not the benefit of the fluid.","Sphincter opening fills capillaries and does not neutralise toxin."],"k":"Exudate dilutes pathogen toxins.","rn":[["","Why is exudation and fluid release beneficial?","Why is exudation and fluid release beneficial?","Dilutes pathogen toxins"]]},{"id":44073,"t":"Acute to Chronic Inflammation","s":"Which plasma protein in exudate is the precursor of a fibrous protein that traps microbes at an infection site and is involved in coagulation and wound healing?","a":["fibrinogen"],"e":"Fibrinogen in the exudate is a precursor of fibrin, a fibrous protein involved in blood coagulation and wound healing. It forms a fibre-rich network at the site of infection that can trap microbes. This is one of the local benefits of increased permeability and exudation.","k":"Fibrinogen → fibrin network traps microbes.","rn":[["","Fibrinogen is the precursor of what?","Fibrinogen is the precursor of what?","Fibrin"]]},{"id":44074,"t":"What Is Inflammation","s":"Which is <b>not</b> one of the three possible outcomes of acute inflammation?","o":["Reversion to resting state by adaptive antibody alone with no clearance of debris","Complete resolution with all debris and pathogens cleared","Healing by tissue replacement","Progression to chronic inflammation","Resolution followed by wound healing after tissue damage"],"e":"The three possible outcomes are complete resolution, healing by tissue replacement and progression to chronic inflammation. Wound healing follows resolution where tissue has been damaged. Successful resolution means the busy environment quietens with debris and pathogens all cleared, so a state with debris left behind does not fit.","w":["Complete resolution is the successful outcome.","Healing by tissue replacement is a listed outcome.","Progression to chronic inflammation is a listed outcome.","Wound healing does follow resolution, so this is a valid path."],"k":"Outcomes: resolution, healing by replacement, progression to chronic.","rn":[["","How do dendritic cells link innate to adaptive immunity?","How do dendritic cells link innate to adaptive immunity?","Carry and present pathogens to lymphocytes; Activate antibody and T-cell responses"]]},{"id":44075,"t":"Acute to Chronic Inflammation","s":"A patient develops an autoimmune disease with inflammation that was chronic from the very start and never had an obvious acute stage. What is this pattern called?","o":["Primary chronic inflammation","Secondary acute inflammation","Complete resolution","Abscess formation from an acute response","Systemic inflammatory response syndrome"],"e":"Chronic inflammation can develop without a prior acute phase, which is called primary chronic inflammation. Examples are tuberculosis, autoimmune disease such as rheumatoid arthritis, chronic inflammatory bowel disease and necrotic adipose tissue. It can also result from repeated events of acute inflammation.","w":["This is not a recognised category, and the condition was chronic from the start.","Resolution means the inflammation has ended.","An abscess is an example of acute going on to chronic.","This syndrome is a septic-shock-like syndrome from non-infectious causes."],"k":"Primary chronic inflammation has no prior acute phase.","rn":[["","What is primary chronic inflammation?","What is primary chronic inflammation?","No prior acute phase"],["","What are 4 examples of primary chronic inflammation?","What are 4 examples of primary chronic inflammation?","Tuberculosis; Autoimmune disease (rheumatoid arthritis); Chronic inflammatory bowel disease; Necrotic adipose tissue"]]},{"id":44076,"t":"Acute to Chronic Inflammation","s":"Which triad best characterises chronic inflammation?","o":["Angiogenesis, infiltration of more leukocytes, and fibrosis","Vasodilation, oedema, and neutrophil-only infiltration","Stasis, rolling, and transmigration","Complete resolution, tissue replacement, and normal function","Rubor, calor, and tumor with no cellular changes in the tissue"],"e":"Chronic inflammation is characterised by angiogenesis (new blood vessel formation), infiltration of more leukocytes, and fibrosis (scarring). Scarring of internal organs can impair their function. The acute pattern, by contrast, centres on vascular changes and neutrophil recruitment.","w":["This describes the acute vascular and neutrophil response.","These are steps of neutrophil recruitment.","These describe a successful acute outcome rather than chronic inflammation.","These are local signs, and chronic inflammation does have distinct cellular changes."],"k":"Chronic = angiogenesis, leukocyte infiltrate, fibrosis.","rn":[["","What changes drive chronic inflammation when resolution fails?","What changes drive chronic inflammation when resolution fails?","Angiogenesis; Leukocyte infiltration; Fibrosis (scarring)"]]},{"id":44077,"t":"Acute to Chronic Inflammation","s":"A lung biopsy from a patient with months of inflammation shows lymphocytes, plasma cells and macrophages rather than mainly neutrophils. Why have these cells appeared?","o":["There has been no resolution, so the adaptive immune response is now involved","Neutrophils live longer than other cells and later transform directly into lymphocytes","Oedema fluid has carried plasma cells from the liver, where they are normally produced","Chronic inflammation involves only the innate immune system and excludes adaptive cells","The biopsy has been taken within hours of the injury, before neutrophils could arrive"],"e":"Chronic inflammation is characterised by increased lymphocytes, plasma cells (antibody-secreting lymphocytes) and macrophages. The adaptive immune response is now involved because there is no resolution. Inflammatory cells themselves cause tissue damage, and necrotic tissue can be present when clearing of debris is not efficient.","w":["Neutrophils are short-lived and do not transform into lymphocytes.","The liver makes complement factors and opsonins, not plasma cells in this response.","Lymphocytes and plasma cells show the adaptive response is involved.","Months of inflammation does not fit a sample taken within hours of an injury."],"k":"Chronic infiltrate reflects adaptive immune involvement with no resolution.","rn":[["","Why do plasma cells appear in chronic inflammation?","Why do plasma cells appear in chronic inflammation?","No resolution brings adaptive immune response in"]]},{"id":44078,"t":"Acute to Chronic Inflammation","s":"In chronic inflammation, what does the persistence of inflammation mean for wound healing?","o":["Repair and angiogenesis start before inflammation has fully resolved","Repair is completely prevented until every inflammatory cell has left","Repair is replaced by the acute neutrophil response for the rest of the illness","Repair happens only in the opposite direction, removing all scar tissue","Repair is done by neutrophils instead of by fibroblasts"],"e":"In chronic inflammation, tissue repair mechanisms and angiogenesis are initiated, so wound healing starts before inflammation has fully resolved. Inflammatory cells also cause tissue damage at the same time. As a result, fibrosis can follow and lead to loss of function.","w":["Repair is initiated despite ongoing inflammation.","Lymphocytes, plasma cells and macrophages characterise this stage rather than a sustained neutrophil-only response.","Scar formation, or fibrosis, is the outcome rather than its removal.","Fibroblasts are the cells that lay down tissue in repair."],"k":"Chronic inflammation: repair begins before inflammation has resolved.","rn":[["","What happens to tissue repair while inflammation is unresolved?","What happens to tissue repair while inflammation is unresolved?","Repair and angiogenesis start early"],["","What 4 features characterise the cellular changes in chronic inflammation?","What 4 features characterise the cellular changes in chronic inflammation?","More lymphocytes, plasma cells, macrophages; Necrotic tissue may be present; Inflammatory cells damage tissue; Tissue repair and angiogenesis begin"]]},{"id":44079,"t":"Acute to Chronic Inflammation","s":"A patient has lost function in an internal organ after years of chronic inflammation, although the original inflammatory stimulus has gone. What process best explains this?","o":["Fibrosis, meaning scarring of the tissue","Vasodilation of arterioles in the organ","Formation of exudate rich in plasma proteins","Stasis in the capillary network","Rapid decay of mediators with short half-lives"],"e":"Fibrosis is scarring of the tissue and it characterises chronic inflammation. Most people have a small scar on the skin, but scarring of internal organs can impair their function. Healing with fibrosis can result in loss of function even after the injury has passed.","w":["Vasodilation is an early acute vascular change.","Exudate formation is an acute vascular change with local benefits.","Stasis helps neutrophils reach the vessel wall.","Rapid mediator decay helps to terminate the response."],"k":"Internal scarring (fibrosis) can impair organ function.","rn":[["","Why does fibrosis in internal organs matter?","Why does fibrosis in internal organs matter?","Scarring impairs organ function"]]},{"id":44080,"t":"Mediators of Inflammation","s":"Which cytokine set is characteristic of <b>chronic</b> inflammation?","o":["Interleukin 12, interferon gamma and interleukin 17","Tumour necrosis factor, interleukin 1 and interleukin 6","Interleukin 1, histamine and interleukin 12","Tumour necrosis factor, prostaglandins and interleukin 6","Interleukin 6, C3b and leukotrienes"],"e":"Interleukin 12, interferon gamma and interleukin 17 are listed for chronic inflammation. Tumour necrosis factor, interleukin 1, interleukin 6 and chemokines are listed for acute inflammation. Histamine, prostaglandins, C3b and leukotrienes are not cytokines.","w":["These are the acute inflammation cytokines.","Histamine is not a cytokine, and interleukin 1 belongs to the acute set.","Prostaglandins are lipids, and the others belong to the acute set.","C3b is a complement fragment and leukotrienes are lipid mediators."],"k":"Chronic cytokines: IL-12, IFN-γ, IL-17.","rn":[["","What does IFN-γ (interferon gamma) do?","What does IFN-γ (interferon gamma) do?","Activates macrophages; Better killing of microbes, tumour cells"]]},{"id":44081,"t":"Mediators of Inflammation","s":"A dendritic cell in a chronic lesion releases interleukin 12. Which chain of events then enhances killing of microbes and tumour cells?","o":["Increased interferon gamma from T and NK cells, which activates macrophages","Increased interleukin 6 from macrophages, which directly lyses tumour cells","Increased interleukin 17 from dendritic cells, which directly activates fibroblasts","Decreased interferon gamma, which removes a brake on macrophages at the site","Increased histamine from basophils, which activates macrophages through H2 receptors"],"e":"Interleukin 12 from dendritic cells and macrophages increases production of interferon gamma. Interferon gamma comes from T lymphocytes and natural killer cells. It activates macrophages, increasing their ability to kill microbes and tumour cells. Interleukin 17, from T lymphocytes, instead recruits neutrophils and monocytes.","w":["Interleukin 6 is listed for systemic effects rather than this route.","Interleukin 17 comes from T lymphocytes and recruits neutrophils and monocytes.","Interleukin 12 increases rather than decreases interferon gamma.","Histamine acts on vessels, and the macrophage-activating cytokine is interferon gamma."],"k":"IL-12 → IFN-γ → activated macrophages.","rn":[["","What does IFN-γ (interferon gamma) do?","What does IFN-γ (interferon gamma) do?","Activates macrophages; Better killing of microbes, tumour cells"]]},{"id":44082,"t":"Mediators of Inflammation","s":"Which interleukin, made by T lymphocytes, recruits neutrophils and monocytes in chronic inflammation?","a":["interleukin 17","interleukin-17","il-17","il17","il 17"],"e":"Interleukin 17 comes from T lymphocytes and recruits neutrophils and monocytes in chronic inflammation. Interleukin 12 raises interferon gamma, and interferon gamma activates macrophages. Together these three cytokines keep the chronic response going.","k":"IL-17 (T cells) recruits neutrophils and monocytes.","rn":[["","Which cells make IL-6 (interleukin 6)?","Which cells make IL-6 (interleukin 6)?","Macrophages; Other cells"],["","What does IL stand for?","What does IL stand for?","Interleukin"],["","Which cells make IL-1 (interleukin 1)?","Which cells make IL-1 (interleukin 1)?","Macrophages; Endothelial cells; Some epithelial cells"]]},{"id":44083,"t":"Mediators of Inflammation","s":"Comparing the acute cytokines, which statement is correct?","o":["Interleukin 1 acts similarly to tumour necrosis factor but has a greater role in fever","Tumour necrosis factor has no effect on endothelial adhesion molecules at all","Interleukin 6 is the main recruiter of leukocytes to normal tissues","Chemokines come from macrophages only and act only in chronic inflammation","Interleukin 1 comes from T lymphocytes only and has no systemic effects"],"e":"Tumour necrosis factor stimulates expression of endothelial adhesion molecules and secretion of other cytokines, and it has systemic effects. Interleukin 1 acts similarly with a greater role in fever. Interleukin 6 has systemic effects. Chemokines recruit leukocytes to sites of inflammation and also control migration in normal tissues.","w":["Tumour necrosis factor stimulates expression of selectins and integrins.","Recruitment of leukocytes is the chemokine role.","Chemokines have many sources and are listed under acute inflammation.","Interleukin 1 comes from macrophages, endothelial cells and some epithelial cells."],"k":"IL-1 ≈ TNF, with a greater role in fever.","rn":[["","How does IL-1 differ from TNF?","How does IL-1 differ from TNF?","Similar actions; Greater role in fever"]]},{"id":44084,"t":"Acute to Chronic Inflammation","s":"A lung biopsy shows macrophages that resemble epithelial cells, large cells with multiple nuclei, lymphocytes and a central necrotic area. What is the process?","o":["Granulomatous inflammation","Acute suppurative abscess","Normal resolution of acute inflammation","Septic shock","Endothelial retraction"],"e":"Granulomatous inflammation is a form of chronic inflammation characterised by macrophages, lymphocytes and central necrosis. Epithelioid cells are activated macrophages that begin to resemble epithelial cells. Giant cells are macrophages that fuse to form cells with multiple nuclei. In highly functional organs such as the lungs, it can impair function.","w":["An abscess is pus with accumulated neutrophils rather than this macrophage pattern.","Resolution leaves a quiet environment with debris cleared.","Septic shock is a severe cytokine-driven syndrome from severe bacterial infection.","Endothelial retraction is a short-lived vascular leak mechanism."],"k":"Granuloma: macrophages, epithelioid and giant cells, lymphocytes, central necrosis.","rn":[["","What characterises granulomatous inflammation?","What characterises granulomatous inflammation?","Macrophages; Lymphocytes; Central necrosis"],["","Image card: What type of inflammation is this? What characterises it?","What type of inflammation is this? What characterises it?","Granulomatous inflammation; Macrophages, lymphocytes, central necrosis"]]},{"id":44085,"t":"Acute to Chronic Inflammation","s":"Which condition is <b>not</b> listed as a cause of granulomatous inflammation?","o":["A brief histamine-mediated skin reaction","Tuberculosis","Leprosy","Crohn disease","A retained suture"],"e":"Granulomatous inflammation is caused by tuberculosis, leprosy, syphilis and Crohn disease, as well as persistent pathogens and foreign bodies such as sutures. A brief histamine reaction resolves in minutes through endothelial retraction and has none of the persistent stimulus needed to form a granuloma.","w":["Tuberculosis is a listed cause.","Leprosy is a listed cause.","Crohn disease is a listed cause.","Foreign bodies such as sutures are a listed cause, as macrophages accumulate around them."],"k":"Granuloma causes: TB, leprosy, syphilis, Crohn disease, foreign bodies, persistent pathogens."},{"id":44086,"t":"Acute to Chronic Inflammation","s":"What are macrophages called once they have fused to form cells with multiple nuclei in a granuloma?","a":["giant cells","giant cell","multinucleate giant cells","multinucleated giant cells"],"e":"Giant cells are macrophages that fuse to form cells with multiple nuclei. Epithelioid cells are activated macrophages resembling epithelial cells. Both are found in granulomatous inflammation, a form of chronic inflammation with overcrowding of macrophages and lymphocytes.","k":"Giant cells = fused macrophages.","rn":[["","What are giant cells?","What are giant cells?","Fused macrophages with multiple nuclei"]]},{"id":44087,"t":"Acute to Chronic Inflammation","s":"Which cell drives the prolonged macrophage recruitment that forms a granuloma around a persistent pathogen?","o":["T cells","Neutrophils","Basophils","Fibroblasts","Mast cells"],"e":"With a persistent pathogen, lymphocytes (T cells) cause prolonged recruitment of macrophages and formation of a granuloma. This explains the crowded structure of macrophages and lymphocytes. Interferon gamma from T lymphocytes also activates macrophages in chronic inflammation.","w":["Neutrophils belong mainly to the acute response and are short-lived.","Basophils are a source of histamine.","Fibroblasts lay down connective tissue.","Mast cells release histamine in the vascular response."],"k":"T cells drive prolonged macrophage recruitment in granulomas.","rn":[["","Which cells prolong macrophage recruitment in a persistent infection?","Which cells prolong macrophage recruitment in a persistent infection?","T cells"]]},{"id":44088,"t":"Mediators of Inflammation","s":"A patient's inflammatory cytokines act at a distant site, increasing the liver's output of proteins such as complement factors and opsonins. Which cytokines are mainly responsible?","o":["Interleukin 1 and interleukin 6","Tumour necrosis factor alone","Interleukin 12 and interleukin 17","Interferon gamma and lipoxins","Histamine and bradykinin acting together"],"e":"In the liver, interleukin 1 and interleukin 6 increase acute phase proteins such as complement factors and opsonins including C-reactive protein. The brain and bone marrow respond to tumour necrosis factor, interleukin 1 and interleukin 6, giving fever and more leukocyte production. Interleukin 6 is the main inducer of C-reactive protein.","w":["The liver effect is listed for interleukin 1 and interleukin 6, not tumour necrosis factor alone.","These are listed under chronic inflammation rather than the liver effect.","Interferon gamma activates macrophages and lipoxins inhibit neutrophil recruitment.","These are vascular mediators, not the cytokines listed for the liver effect."],"k":"Liver acute-phase response: IL-1 and IL-6.","rn":[["","What does IL stand for?","What does IL stand for?","Interleukin"],["","Which cells make IL-6 (interleukin 6)?","Which cells make IL-6 (interleukin 6)?","Macrophages; Other cells"]]},{"id":44089,"t":"Systemic Inflammation","s":"Which effect of acute-phase cytokines is <b>pathological</b> if persistent, rather than protective?","o":["Low cardiac output from tumour necrosis factor","Fever produced through the brain","More acute phase proteins from the liver","More leukocyte production in the bone marrow","A rise in opsonin production to help clear pathogens"],"e":"Protective effects are fever (brain), more acute phase proteins (liver) and more leukocyte production (bone marrow). Pathological effects if persistent are low cardiac output (heart), thrombus formation and increased permeability (blood vessels), and insulin resistance (skeletal muscle and multiple tissues). The same cytokines can help in the short term and harm if they persist.","w":["Fever is listed as protective.","More acute phase proteins are listed as protective.","More leukocyte production is listed as protective.","Opsonins are part of the protective liver response."],"k":"Persistent TNF: low cardiac output, thrombus, permeability, insulin resistance.","rn":[["","Which effects are systemic pathological effects?","Which effects are systemic pathological effects?","Low cardiac output; Thrombus, increased permeability; Insulin resistance"]]},{"id":44090,"t":"Systemic Inflammation","s":"A patient with long-standing inflammation develops insulin resistance in skeletal muscle. Which cytokines are implicated?","o":["Tumour necrosis factor and interleukin 1","Interleukin 6 and interleukin 12","Interleukin 17 and interferon gamma","Interleukin 12 and tumour necrosis factor","Only type I interferons"],"e":"Insulin resistance in multiple tissues, including skeletal muscle, is listed as a pathological effect of persistent tumour necrosis factor and interleukin 1. Low cardiac output and vascular thrombus formation are linked to tumour necrosis factor. This illustrates how acute-phase cytokines can harm distant tissues.","w":["These are not the pair listed for insulin resistance.","These are chronic inflammation cytokines and are not listed for this effect.","Interleukin 12 is not listed for this effect.","Type I interferons contribute to systemic effects, but they are not the listed cause here."],"k":"Insulin resistance: persistent TNF and IL-1.","rn":[["","What does TNF stand for?","What does TNF stand for?","Tumor Necrosis Factor"]]},{"id":44091,"t":"Systemic Inflammation","s":"A patient with severe burns has no evidence of infection, yet develops a fall in blood pressure and a syndrome resembling septic shock. What is the best term?","o":["Systemic inflammatory response syndrome","Septic shock","Primary chronic inflammation","Granulomatous inflammation","Acute histamine reaction"],"e":"Systemic inflammatory response syndrome (SIRS) is a syndrome similar to septic shock caused by non-infectious diseases such as severe burns and trauma. Septic shock is due to sepsis from a severe bacterial infection. The discriminator is therefore whether infection is present.","w":["Septic shock requires sepsis from a severe bacterial infection.","Primary chronic inflammation is a long-term process without a prior acute phase.","A granuloma is a chronic localised structure.","A histamine reaction is rapid and short-lived."],"k":"SIRS: septic-shock-like, but non-infectious (burns, trauma).","rn":[["","What is SIRS? Give 2 causes.","What is SIRS? Give 2 causes.","Systemic Inflammatory Response Syndrome; Severe burns; Trauma"]]},{"id":44092,"t":"Immune Cells in Inflammation","s":"A patient with severe bacterial infection develops a fall in blood pressure, disseminated intravascular coagulation and metabolic abnormalities. What is the central mechanism?","o":["Overproduction of cytokines including tumour necrosis factor and interleukin 1","Underproduction of tumour necrosis factor by exhausted tissue macrophages","A failure of all white cell production caused by direct infection of the bone marrow","Excess lipoxin production, which removes all neutrophils from the blood","Localised release of histamine from a small group of skin mast cells"],"e":"Septic shock results from sepsis due to a severe bacterial infection. Overproduction of cytokines including tumour necrosis factor and interleukin 1 causes a series of severe pathological events. Symptoms include a fall in blood pressure, disseminated intravascular coagulation and metabolic abnormalities.","w":["The problem is overproduction of cytokines rather than underproduction.","The mechanism is cytokine excess, whereas acute-phase cytokines actually raise leukocyte production.","Lipoxins limit neutrophil recruitment and are not the cause of this syndrome.","A local skin mast cell reaction would not explain this systemic syndrome."],"k":"Septic shock = cytokine overproduction in severe bacterial infection.","rn":[["","What does TNF stand for?","What does TNF stand for?","Tumor Necrosis Factor"],["","What molecules released during phagocytosis lead to inflammation then repair?","What molecules released during phagocytosis lead to inflammation then repair?","Cytokines; Growth factors"]]},{"id":44093,"t":"Systemic Inflammation","s":"Besides tumour necrosis factor, interleukin 1 and interleukin 6, which class of cytokines also contributes to systemic effects of the acute phase?","a":["type i interferons","type 1 interferons","type i interferon","type 1 interferon","interferons"],"e":"Systemic effects of acute-phase inflammation are caused by cytokine production in response to pathogen infection. Apart from tumour necrosis factor, interleukin 1 and interleukin 6, type I interferons also contribute. The main three remain the most important to remember.","k":"Type I interferons also contribute to systemic effects.","rn":[["","Apart from TNF, IL-1 and IL-6, what else contributes to systemic effects?","Apart from TNF, IL-1 and IL-6, what else contributes to systemic effects?","Type I interferons"]]},{"id":44094,"t":"Systemic Inflammation","s":"Which sequence correctly describes the production of fever in acute inflammation?","o":["Cytokines induce COX-2, prostaglandins form, hypothalamus releases neurotransmitters, set point rises","Prostaglandins induce cytokines, COX-2 forms, the liver releases neurotransmitters, set point rises","COX-2 induces cytokines, leukotrienes form, the brainstem releases histamine, set point rises","Cytokines induce COX-1, histamine forms, skin mast cells release heat, set point falls","Hypothalamus releases cytokines, COX-2 falls, prostaglandins stop forming, set point rises"],"e":"Tumour necrosis factor, interleukin 1 and interleukin 6, the endogenous pyrogens, induce expression of cyclooxygenase 2 (COX-2). COX-2 stimulates production of prostaglandins. These act on the hypothalamus to release neurotransmitters, which set the body temperature higher. Fever is an increase of between 1°C and 4°C.","w":["The cytokines come first and induce COX-2, and the hypothalamus rather than the liver is involved.","COX-2 is induced by the cytokines, and the product here is prostaglandins, not leukotrienes.","COX-2 is the relevant enzyme, and the set point rises through the hypothalamus.","Cytokines act first, and COX-2 and prostaglandins must rise for fever to occur."],"k":"Pyrogens → COX-2 → prostaglandins → hypothalamus → higher temperature.","rn":[["","Where do prostaglandins act to cause fever?","Where do prostaglandins act to cause fever?","Hypothalamus; Neurotransmitters set temperature higher"]]},{"id":44095,"t":"Systemic Inflammation","s":"A patient with a fever takes aspirin and the temperature falls. Where in the pathway does aspirin act?","o":["Synthesis of prostaglandins","Release of tumour necrosis factor","Binding of interleukin 1 at the liver","Rolling of neutrophils along selectins","Histamine release from basophils"],"e":"Aspirin inhibits the synthesis of prostaglandins, interrupting the cascade by which pyrogens act through COX-2 and the hypothalamus to raise body temperature. This can help with fever symptoms. It does not act at the earlier cytokine stage, according to this pathway.","w":["Aspirin acts downstream of cytokine release.","Liver acute-phase responses are not the target described.","Rolling is a recruitment step and not part of the fever pathway.","Histamine is not the mediator in this fever pathway."],"k":"Aspirin blocks prostaglandin synthesis.","rn":[["","What does aspirin inhibit?","What does aspirin inhibit?","Prostaglandin synthesis"]]},{"id":44096,"t":"Systemic Inflammation","s":"What term describes substances, such as tumour necrosis factor, interleukin 1 and interleukin 6, that induce fever?","a":["pyrogens","pyrogen","endogenous pyrogens","endogenous pyrogen"],"e":"Fever is an increase in body temperature of between 1°C and 4°C induced by pyrogens. Tumour necrosis factor, interleukin 1 and interleukin 6 are the endogenous pyrogens. They induce COX-2, which leads to prostaglandin production and action on the hypothalamus.","k":"Pyrogens induce fever by a rise of 1–4°C.","rn":[["","What induces fever?","What induces fever?","Pyrogens"],["","Endogenous pyrogens induce expression of which enzyme?","Endogenous pyrogens induce expression of which enzyme?","COX-2"]]},{"id":44097,"t":"Systemic Inflammation","s":"What distinguishes COX-2 from COX-1 in relation to inflammation?","o":["COX-2 expression is confined to cells participating in inflammatory reactions","COX-2 alone produces leukotrienes from histidine in the mast cell granules","COX-2 is a plasma protein made in the liver and acts as an opsonin","COX-2 is the only cyclooxygenase involved in generating prostaglandins","COX-2 inhibits pyrogens and so prevents the rise in body temperature"],"e":"Prostaglandins are generated by two cyclooxygenases, COX-1 and COX-2. COX-2 expression is confined to cells participating in inflammatory reactions. The endogenous pyrogens induce COX-2, which is how fever is initiated. Prostaglandins are products of arachidonic acid and are involved in pain and fever.","w":["Histidine is the precursor of histamine, and leukotrienes come from arachidonic acid.","Opsonins such as C-reactive protein are the plasma proteins from the liver.","Both COX-1 and COX-2 generate prostaglandins.","Pyrogens induce COX-2, and its action contributes to fever rather than preventing it."],"k":"COX-2 is confined to cells in inflammatory reactions.","rn":[["","Endogenous pyrogens induce expression of which enzyme?","Endogenous pyrogens induce expression of which enzyme?","COX-2"]]},{"id":44098,"t":"Wound Healing Phases","s":"In a deep wound with no antibiotics available, the first phase occurs in the intact vessels around and beneath the wound. What is this phase, and what does it produce?","o":["The vascular phase, in which vessels dilate and leak, producing the classic signs","The neutrophil phase, in which the wound edge is sealed by collagen immediately","The macrophage phase, in which granulation tissue fills in the wound from below","The angiofibro phase, in which myofibroblasts contract the new scar and close the wound","The contraction phase, in which the wound pulls together before any white cells enter"],"e":"The vascular phase is first and happens to the intact vessels and structures around and beneath the wound. Vessels dilate and become more permeable, letting white blood cells escape. Resident cells detect the injury and signal back. This phase accounts for the swelling, warmth, redness and pain.","w":["The neutrophil phase comes second and is best thought of as combat with bacteria.","The macrophage phase is about clearing up and coordinating repair.","The angiofibro phase is the repair stage later on.","Contraction is at the very end, not the first step."],"k":"Phase 1, vascular: dilation and leak produce the classic signs.","rn":[["","Vascular phase of wound response: what happens?","Vascular phase of wound response: what happens?","Intact vessels near wound dilate; Become more permeable"]]},{"id":44099,"t":"What Is Inflammation","s":"A wound is clearly swollen, warm, red and painful a short time after injury. What is the most appropriate interpretation?","o":["This is a normal response: the body is reacting, and usually to bacteria in the wound","This always means a systemic infection has taken hold, and sepsis can be diagnosed from it","This means the wound has reached the angiofibro stage, so bacteria have already been cleared","This means that the neutrophils have all died and that the wound will not heal any further","This means the response is purely to the physical gap in tissue, with no role for bacteria"],"e":"Swelling comes from vascular permeability, warmth and redness from vasodilation, and pain from white-cell compounds exciting local nerves. Seeing these is not a bad thing, because it means the body is responding. The response is usually not to the physical wound but to the bacteria in it. These defences evolved to deal with bacterial attack through wounds.","w":["Local signs alone do not prove that bacteria have overwhelmed defences.","The angiofibro stage is recognised by granulation tissue, not by these early signs.","These signs reflect the active vascular response, not neutrophil death.","The response is usually to bacteria in the wound rather than to the physical gap."],"k":"Early local signs mean the body is responding, usually to bacteria.","rn":[["","Why is inflammation in a wound often a response to bacteria, not just the cut?","Why is inflammation in a wound often a response to bacteria, not just the cut?","Wounds almost always contain bacteria; Defences evolved to counter bacterial attack"]]},{"id":44100,"t":"Wound Healing Phases","s":"In the neutrophil phase of a wound, what are the three outcomes of the fight between neutrophils and bacteria?","o":["Neutrophils win, bacteria win (sepsis risk), or a draw","Neutrophils win, macrophages win, or fibroblasts win","Bacteria win, granulation occurs, or contraction occurs","Complete healing, an abscess, or a surgical wound","Neutrophils win, bacteria die alone, or antibiotics win"],"e":"If neutrophils win, inflammation carries on normally to heal the wound. If bacteria win, they overwhelm defences and enter the circulation, triggering a systemic response, often called sepsis. A draw is a stalemate that also moves on to the next phase. This frames what happens in the later phases.","w":["Macrophages and fibroblasts are not opposing sides in this fight.","Granulation and contraction are later healing processes.","These are different kinds of end result rather than outcomes of the fight.","Bacteria do not simply die alone, and antibiotics are not a named outcome of the fight."],"k":"Fight outcomes: neutrophils win, bacteria win, or a draw.","rn":[["","What are the 3 outcomes of neutrophils vs bacteria?","What are the 3 outcomes of neutrophils vs bacteria?","Neutrophils win; Bacteria win; Draw"]]},{"id":44101,"t":"Wound Healing Phases","s":"An elderly patient has poor nutrition and a wound on a limb with poor blood supply. Why are they at increased risk of bacteria winning?","o":["Fewer neutrophils can reach the wound, and those that do have little \"ammunition\"","Excess neutrophils live for many years and exhaust the wound's macrophage supply","Poor blood supply and nutrition make fibroblasts convert into bacteria-friendly cells","Low nutrition raises the skin barrier, trapping neutrophils outside of the wound","Reduced blood flow speeds healing, closing the wound prematurely over any bacteria"],"e":"Bacteria may win if defences are not good enough. Low nutritional levels mean neutrophils do not have much ammunition. Poor blood supply to a part of the body, such as a limb, means enough troops cannot be transported to the front. Both weaken the response at the wound.","w":["Neutrophils are short-lived and are not said to exhaust macrophages.","Fibroblasts are connective tissue cells and do not become bacteria-friendly cells.","Low nutrition reduces neutrophil ammunition rather than raising a barrier.","Poor blood supply impairs delivery rather than speeding healing."],"k":"Poor nutrition and poor blood supply let bacteria win.","rn":[["","How does an aseptic surgical wound heal?","How does an aseptic surgical wound heal?","By same inflammation, much milder; No bacteria: neutrophils have little to do"]]},{"id":44102,"t":"Wound Healing Phases","s":"After the neutrophil phase, which cell coordinates the clean-up and repair and is attracted as a second line of defence?","a":["macrophage","macrophages"],"e":"The macrophage phase follows if the body wins or draws. It is a phase of clearing up and repairing, coordinated by the macrophage, which is attracted by neutrophils in the area. The battleground is full of dead and dying neutrophils and bacteria. Macrophages clear this material and also have a coordination role.","k":"Macrophages clear debris and coordinate repair.","rn":[["","Macrophage phase: what do macrophages do?","Macrophage phase: what do macrophages do?","Clear dead neutrophils and bacteria; Coordinate repair; Attracted by neutrophils"]]},{"id":44103,"t":"Wound Healing Phases","s":"Why is the mass of dead and dying cells in a wound dangerous if it is left there?","o":["It is terrible for neutrophils but great for bacteria, which thrive there","It is terrible for bacteria but great for neutrophils, which feed on it","It stops fibroblasts from ever forming new connective tissue in the body","It secretes aspirin-like molecules that block prostaglandin production","It keeps all blood vessels permanently closed around the wound"],"e":"The battleground fills with dead and dying neutrophils and bacteria. This is terrible for neutrophils but great for bacteria, which are adapted to thrive there. Macrophages therefore clear the dead material. A trapped core of dead material can allow bacteria to proliferate, as in an abscess.","w":["This reverses the effect, because bacteria are the ones that thrive there.","Fibroblasts still come in later in the repair stage.","Dead material does not make aspirin-like molecules.","Vessels are dilated and leaky around a wound rather than closed."],"k":"Dead material favours bacteria, so it must be cleared.","rn":[["","Why is a battlefield of dead neutrophils good for bacteria?","Why is a battlefield of dead neutrophils good for bacteria?","Bacteria thrive among dead material"]]},{"id":44104,"t":"Wound Healing Phases","s":"In a long-term \"draw\", a core of dead material becomes surrounded by macrophages for weeks to months. What is this structure?","a":["granuloma","a granuloma","granulomas"],"e":"A long-term draw resembles a trench standstill: a core of dead material surrounded by macrophages. This is a granuloma, a form of chronic inflammation that develops over weeks to many months because the bacteria are hard to beat. It is associated with tuberculosis and leprosy. It traps the bacteria, but imperfectly.","k":"Long-term draw = granuloma.","rn":[["","What is a long-term draw between neutrophils and bacteria?","What is a long-term draw between neutrophils and bacteria?","Ring of macrophages around dead core; Granuloma: chronic inflammation"]]},{"id":44105,"t":"Wound Healing Phases","s":"Which statement correctly contrasts a long-term draw, a short-term draw and an outright win?","o":["Long-term forms a granuloma; short-term pushes debris out over days to weeks; a win lets the macrophage ring collapse","Long-term heals in days; short-term forms a granuloma for months; a win leaves a macrophage ring in place for good","Long-term produces sepsis; short-term produces a granuloma; a win means bacteria reach the circulation","Long-term needs surgery; short-term needs antibiotics; a win always requires debridement","Long-term has no macrophages; short-term has no neutrophils; a win has no repair stage"],"e":"In a long-term draw, a granuloma forms over weeks to many months. In a short-term draw, neutrophils win slowly, and the macrophage-neutrophil alliance pushes debris out over days or weeks. If neutrophils win outright, macrophages come in at the end to clear the mess, so the ring collapses rather than staying.","w":["The granuloma is the long-term draw, and the ring collapses in a win.","Sepsis is due to bacteria winning, not due to a draw.","Wounds do not have fixed treatments by type of draw.","Macrophages surround the core in a long-term draw, and neutrophils are key in a short-term one."],"k":"Long-term draw: granuloma. Short-term: slow push-out. Win: ring collapses.","rn":[["","What happens in a short-term draw?","What happens in a short-term draw?","Macrophage-neutrophil alliance pushes debris out slowly; Repair phase begins behind them"],["","What is a long-term draw between neutrophils and bacteria?","What is a long-term draw between neutrophils and bacteria?","Ring of macrophages around dead core; Granuloma: chronic inflammation"]]},{"id":44106,"t":"Wound Healing Phases","s":"A patient's graze has a scab, and beneath it there is glistening pink tissue that bleeds easily when rubbed. What does this tissue indicate?","o":["Granulation tissue, showing the repair stage has begun and the body is winning","An abscess, showing the wound has closed over bacteria and is getting worse","Necrotic tissue, showing the neutrophils have died and that the wound is lost","Epithelioid tissue, showing a granuloma has formed as a form of chronic inflammation","Exudate, showing that the vascular phase has only just started at the wound"],"e":"Granulation tissue is new tissue that is very vascular, quite fibrous, pink and smooth, and it bleeds easily if rubbed. It glistens under a scab after a graze or burn. Seeing it means you are in phase 4, the repair stage, and definitely winning. New vessels and fibroblasts have moved in to fill the gap.","w":["An abscess would be red, hot and swollen under a seemingly healed top.","Necrotic tissue is dead material rather than new vascular tissue.","Epithelioid cells are activated macrophages in granulomas, not this surface tissue.","Exudate is fluid leaking from vessels, which is not solid pink tissue."],"k":"Glistening, pink, bleeding-prone tissue = granulation tissue, winning.","rn":[["","Which phase does granulation tissue show you are in?","Which phase does granulation tissue show you are in?","Phase four (repair), winning"],["","What are 3 features of granulation tissue?","What are 3 features of granulation tissue?","Very vascular; Pink and smooth; Bleeds easily"]]},{"id":44107,"t":"Wound Healing Phases","s":"Which two components come in during the angiofibro repair stage of a wound?","o":["New blood vessels and fibroblasts","Neutrophils and bacteria","Mast cells and platelets","Plasma cells and basophils","Epithelioid cells and giant cells"],"e":"In phase 4, new blood vessels and fibroblasts, which are connective tissue cells and not white cells, come in to lay down tissue and push the gap shut. This is why it is called angiofibro. Granulation tissue is the new, very vascular and fibrous tissue that forms. Wound contraction follows.","w":["Neutrophils and bacteria are the sides of the phase 2 combat.","Mast cells release histamine, and platelets are not the components named here.","Plasma cells and basophils are not the repair components in this stage.","These cells are features of a granuloma rather than this stage."],"k":"Angiofibro stage: new vessels and fibroblasts.","rn":[["","Angiofibro (repair) phase: what happens?","Angiofibro (repair) phase: what happens?","New blood vessels form; Fibroblasts lay down tissue"]]},{"id":44108,"t":"Wound Healing Phases","s":"A wide wound heals with a slightly puckered appearance. Which cell type is mainly responsible for that appearance?","a":["myofibroblast","myofibroblasts"],"e":"In the last phase, some fibroblasts develop muscular fibres, becoming myofibroblasts, which contract the wound and close it. A wide wound often ends up slightly puckered because of this contraction. So the gap fills in, the wound contracts and the skin grows over.","k":"Myofibroblasts contract the wound; wide wounds pucker.","rn":[["","How do fibroblasts close a wide wound?","How do fibroblasts close a wide wound?","Develop myofibroblast contractile fibres; Contract and pucker the wound"]]},{"id":44109,"t":"Wound Healing Phases","s":"A wound's skin appears healed, but a week later the site is red, hot and swollen again. What is the most likely explanation?","o":["The top closed before infection cleared, trapping dead material where bacteria multiply","The wound has completed healing, and those signs now show normal skin regrowth at the top","Fibroblasts are producing a new round of neutrophils under the fully healed skin","The scab has gone, and that absence on its own is the cause of the vascular phase","The wound contracted too strongly, cutting all blood supply to the top layer of the skin"],"e":"In a wound abscess, the skin and top of the wound close before all infection is cleared, leaving dead material underneath. Bacteria proliferate in the trapped core and the whole cycle repeats. A new vascular phase makes it red, hot and swollen beneath what looks healed. You thought you were winning, then started losing again.","w":["These signs show active inflammation rather than normal completion of healing.","Fibroblasts do not produce neutrophils.","The scab is the least important part, and it is what lies beneath that matters.","Contraction closes wounds but is not the cause of the renewed vascular phase."],"k":"Abscess: top closes first, infection trapped beneath.","rn":[["","How does a wound abscess form?","How does a wound abscess form?","Top heals first; Bacteria multiply in trapped debris"]]},{"id":44110,"t":"Wound Healing Phases","s":"Why does a gunshot wound classically lead to an abscess?","o":["The small entry closes over a lot of damage below, with clothing as continuing irritation","The hot bullet feeds the bacteria, so it provides all of the nutrients for them to grow","The bullet has a large entry wound that stays open and so drains itself of the bacteria","The metal bullet stimulates neutrophils, so bacteria are destroyed before they multiply","The bleeding from a bullet track is so heavy that the clotting cascade never occurs at all"],"e":"An abscess often arises when a wound is small but leaves a lot of damage below, for example a gunshot wound. What remains is the piece of clothing, which is the continuing irritation. The bullet is hot and metal, so there is not much there for bacteria. The small top closes before the damage below has been cleared.","w":["The bullet is hot and metal, so there is not much there for bacteria.","An abscess occurs when the top closes, and a self-draining wound would not form one.","Bacteria are still able to multiply in the trapped dead material.","Clotting problems are not the stated reason for the abscess."],"k":"Small wound, large damage below, retained clothing → abscess."},{"id":44111,"t":"Wound Healing Phases","s":"A wound with an abscess is opened by removing its closed-over top. What is the purpose of this intervention?","o":["To make a path so the alliance can keep pushing material out","To kill all neutrophils so the bacteria have nothing to fight","To stop macrophages from entering, and so prevent scar formation","To promote the growth of bacteria so they can be cultured easily","To close the wound over the top of any remaining infected debris"],"e":"Draining the abscess means taking away the closed-over top. This makes a path so the neutrophil-macrophage alliance can carry on pushing material out. Healing then finishes secondarily, after an abscess has formed and been drained. The aim is to help the body's defences rather than replace them.","w":["Neutrophils are allies in clearing the infection.","Macrophages are needed for clearing up and coordinating repair.","The purpose is to clear infected material, not to grow bacteria.","Closing over debris is the very problem that produces an abscess."],"k":"Drainage opens a path for debris to be pushed out.","rn":[["","How does draining or debridement help?","How does draining or debridement help?","Lets neutrophil-macrophage alliance push on; Removes debris"]]},{"id":44112,"t":"Wound Healing Phases","s":"What is the term for physically removing dead material and debris from a wound, considered the most effective intervention?","a":["debridement","debriding","wound debridement"],"e":"Debridement means physically removing the rubbish beneath the wound surface. It can be done earlier, before an abscess forms, and repeatedly. It tips the balance in the alliance's favour and is regarded as the most effective intervention. Any standard doctor, not just a surgeon, can do it.","k":"Debridement tips the balance and is the most effective intervention.","rn":[["","What is debridement?","What is debridement?","Physically removing wound debris"]]},{"id":44113,"t":"Wound Healing Phases","s":"A ward doctor considers packing a dirty wound with an antiseptic-soaked \"healing\" compound instead of cleaning it. What is the most appropriate advice?","o":["Do not stuff it with compounds: debride it and let the body heal itself","Stuff it with compounds daily, as they replace the need for neutrophils","Leave the debris in place, because it feeds the macrophages at the site","Close the top immediately so that nothing can leave the wound afterwards","Refer every wound to a surgeon, as only surgeons can clean wounds safely"],"e":"Stuffing a wound with magical compounds can make it worse. The right approach is to debride and let the body heal itself, which it will do in most cases. On the ward, make sure a wound is clean with the muck out of it. Hands and tools are the most effective thing, and any standard doctor can do this.","w":["Compounds can make things worse and do not replace the body's defences.","Dead material is great for bacteria, so it should be removed.","Closing the top over debris sets up an abscess.","Any standard doctor, not only a surgeon, can clean a wound."],"k":"Don't pack wounds with compounds; debride and let the body heal."},{"id":44114,"t":"Wound Healing Phases","s":"In which patient are antibiotics most justified for a wound?","o":["A patient with a heavily contaminated dog bite, losing from the start","A healthy patient with a clean graze that already shows granulation tissue","A patient with a clean surgical wound that has been closed with stitches","A healthy patient with redness and warmth just after a small clean cut","A patient whose wound is contracting and puckering at the end of healing"],"e":"Antibiotics are not a front-line treatment for wounds, because the body is adapted to heal them. They are for people with sepsis or impending sepsis, or those with weak defences. They are also for wounds that are heavily contaminated, where you think you are losing from the start, such as dog bites.","w":["Granulation tissue means the patient is winning, so antibiotics are not front-line.","Aseptic surgical wounds have no bacteria introduced and move quickly to later stages.","Early local signs are a normal response rather than a reason for antibiotics.","Contraction is the last phase of normal healing."],"k":"Antibiotics: sepsis, weak defences, heavy contamination (e.g. dog bites).","rn":[["","When are antibiotics used in wounds?","When are antibiotics used in wounds?","Sepsis or impending sepsis; Weak defences; Heavily contaminated (e.g. dog bite)"]]},{"id":44115,"t":"Wound Healing Phases","s":"Why does a stitched aseptic surgical wound move quickly to the later phases of healing?","o":["No bacteria were introduced, so neutrophils arrive and find nothing to fight","There is no inflammation at all, so healing starts with the contraction phase","Stitches provide nutrients that replace the need for any kind of granulation","Surgical wounds are too small for neutrophils to reach, so they cannot enter","Aseptic conditions kill fibroblasts, so the macrophage phase does all of the repair"],"e":"Aseptic conditions mean no bacteria are introduced. Stitches approximate the edges, so there is a line to heal rather than a crater, and the skin does not need to grow over. It still heals by inflammation, but much less: neutrophils arrive, find nothing to fight, and you move almost immediately to stages 3 and 4.","w":["It still heals by inflammation, just much less.","Stitches approximate edges rather than providing nutrients.","Neutrophils do arrive, and they find nothing to fight.","Fibroblasts are still needed in the angiofibro stage."],"k":"Aseptic surgery shortcuts the phases: little to fight, quick to stages 3 and 4.","rn":[["","How does an aseptic surgical wound heal?","How does an aseptic surgical wound heal?","By same inflammation, much milder; No bacteria: neutrophils have little to do"],["","Neutrophil phase: who fights whom?","Neutrophil phase: who fights whom?","Neutrophils vs bacteria; Both use many mediators"]]},{"id":44116,"t":"Wound Healing Phases","s":"Which option describes the difference between primary and secondary finishing of wound healing in this model?","o":["Primary heals bottom up as skin grows over; secondary follows an abscess that is drained","Primary follows a drained abscess; secondary heals bottom up as skin grows over the gap","Primary uses only stitches; secondary is defined by the use of antibiotics in every case","Primary occurs only in the liver; secondary occurs in all other tissues of the body","Primary means no neutrophils at all; secondary means no macrophages at all"],"e":"Wound healing finishes either primarily or secondarily. In primary finishing, healing goes bottom up and the skin grows over. In secondary finishing, an abscess forms, is drained, and then reaches the final stage. The key difference is whether an abscess has interrupted the process.","w":["This reverses the two routes.","The definition does not depend on the use of stitches or antibiotics.","Both refer to the finishing of a wound, not to a specific organ.","Both routes involve neutrophils and macrophages."],"k":"Primary: bottom up. Secondary: abscess, drainage, final stage.","rn":[["","What are the 2 routes to final healing of wounds?","What are the 2 routes to final healing of wounds?","Primary: heal from bottom, skin grows over; Secondary: abscess forms, drained"]]},{"id":44117,"t":"Wound Healing Phases","s":"A patient worries because a scab has fallen off a healing graze, revealing moist pink tissue beneath. Which statement is correct?","o":["The scab is the least important part, and what matters is the tissue underneath","The scab is the main source of neutrophils, so its loss stops inflammation entirely","The scab secretes the fibroblasts that fill the wound from the bottom upwards","Loss of the scab always means the wound has become a deep abscess at that moment","The scab provides all of the blood supply that the granulation tissue will require"],"e":"The scab is the least important part, and what matters is underneath it. Clotting happens early, and blood clots in a test tube with no cells at all. Glistening pink tissue under a scab is granulation tissue, showing the repair stage is under way and the body is winning.","w":["Neutrophils come from the blood rather than from the scab.","Fibroblasts are connective tissue cells and are not secreted by a scab.","An abscess would be red, hot and swollen under a healed top.","Granulation tissue has its own new vessels growing from below."],"k":"The scab matters least; the tissue beneath shows progress."},{"id":44118,"t":"Wound Healing Phases","s":"Vacuum pumps on wounds and maggots may both be used in wound care. What do these two approaches have in common?","o":["Both debride the wound","Both act as front-line antibiotics","Both seal the top of the wound","Both replace all inflammatory cells","Both stop the formation of any new vessels"],"e":"A vacuum pump acts as a kind of 24-hour debridement, and maggots also debride. Doing it by hand daily is probably just as good. The common aim is to remove dead material so that the balance tips towards the neutrophil-macrophage alliance.","w":["Neither of these is an antibiotic.","Sealing the top would risk an abscess.","They help the defences rather than replacing them.","New vessels are needed for repair, and these approaches do not stop them."],"k":"Vacuum pumps and maggots are forms of debridement.","rn":[["","What is the treatment for an abscess?","What is the treatment for an abscess?","Drain it; Debride the wound"],["","What is debridement?","What is debridement?","Physically removing wound debris"],["","Are antibiotics first-line treatment for wounds?","Are antibiotics first-line treatment for wounds?","No, debridement is key"]]},{"id":44119,"t":"Wound Healing Phases","s":"Which three cell groups should be remembered for the fight and repair of a wound, in order of appearance?","o":["Neutrophils, macrophages and the angiofibroblast alliance","Macrophages, neutrophils and the plasma cell alliance","Lymphocytes, mast cells and platelet alliance","Fibroblasts, neutrophils and the lymphocyte alliance","Basophils, macrophages and the eosinophil alliance"],"e":"The three cell groups to remember are neutrophils, macrophages and the angiofibroblast alliance at the end. Most of the bacteria-fighting is performed by the neutrophil-macrophage alliance. They communicate using many compounds, including chemokines. There is currently no single medication that manipulates these to improve defences.","w":["Neutrophils come before macrophages, and plasma cells are not in this group.","Lymphocytes and mast cells are not the named wound groups.","Fibroblasts arrive at the end rather than first.","Basophils and eosinophils are not the named wound groups."],"k":"Wound cells: neutrophils → macrophages → angiofibroblasts.","rn":[["","How does draining or debridement help?","How does draining or debridement help?","Lets neutrophil-macrophage alliance push on; Removes debris"],["","What happens in a short-term draw?","What happens in a short-term draw?","Macrophage-neutrophil alliance pushes debris out slowly; Repair phase begins behind them"],["","Macrophage phase: what do macrophages do?","Macrophage phase: what do macrophages do?","Clear dead neutrophils and bacteria; Coordinate repair; Attracted by neutrophils"]]},{"id":44120,"t":"Wound Healing Phases","s":"A doctor examines two wounds. Wound X has glistening pink tissue that bleeds easily. Wound Y looks healed, but is red, hot and swollen underneath. What is the correct action for each?","o":["X is winning and needs little intervention; Y needs draining and debridement","X needs draining and debridement; Y is winning and needs little intervention","Both are winning and need no intervention beyond keeping the scabs fully intact","Both need front-line antibiotics first, with drainage reserved for later failures","X needs compounds packed into it; Y needs stitches placed across the healed top"],"e":"Glistening pink bleeding tissue is granulation tissue, showing the patient is winning. Red, hot and swollen tissue under a seemingly healed wound is an abscess, which needs draining and debriding. Telling the two apart at the bedside guides what to do. Antibiotics are not front-line for wounds.","w":["This reverses the two wounds.","Wound Y is an abscess and should be drained, not left alone.","Antibiotics are not front-line treatment for wounds, and drainage is the key step.","Packing compounds can make wounds worse, and stitching would trap the infection."],"k":"Granulation = winning. Abscess = drain and debride.","rn":[["","What is the treatment for an abscess?","What is the treatment for an abscess?","Drain it; Debride the wound"]]},{"id":44121,"t":"Immune Cells in Inflammation","s":"A 3-year-old has had repeated skin infections since infancy. The wounds contain few neutrophils, yet blood neutrophil counts are very high. Microscopy of a blood vessel near an infected wound shows neutrophils rolling along the wall normally. They do not come to a firm stop, however, and they do not enter the tissue. Cytokine levels at the wound are normal.<br><br>Which molecular step is most likely faulty, and what is the expected consequence?","o":["Integrin binding fails, so stable adhesion and tissue entry are both lost","Selectin binding fails, so the cells never slow down on the vessel wall","PECAM-1 fails, so cells adhere firmly and accumulate stuck along the wall","Macrophage cytokine release fails, so no adhesion molecules are expressed","Chemotaxis in tissue fails, so cells cross the vessel and then stop moving"],"e":"Step 1: rolling is mediated by low-affinity selectin bonds and it is intact here, so the defect lies after that stage. Normal cytokine levels also mean the signal to the endothelium is present. Step 2: firm arrest needs integrins to bind integrin ligands strongly, and the failure to stop points to this step. Without stable adhesion, neutrophils cannot reach the PECAM-1 crossing stage, so they stay in the blood and few reach the wound.","w":["Rolling is normal, so selectin binding is working.","A PECAM-1 fault would leave cells firmly adherent, but these cells do not come to a stop.","Cytokine levels at the wound are normal.","The cells do not cross the vessel at all."],"k":"Rolling normal + no firm stop = integrin defect.","rn":[["","Neutrophil recruitment: job of selectins vs integrins vs PECAM-1?","Neutrophil recruitment: job of selectins vs integrins vs PECAM-1?","Selectins: rolling; Integrins: stable adhesion; PECAM-1: transmigration"],["","What stops the rolling of neutrophils?","What stops the rolling of neutrophils?","Integrin ligands binding neutrophil integrins; Strong binding"]]},{"id":44122,"t":"Inflammation and Blood Vessels","s":"A 25-year-old is stung on the arm. Within minutes, the area is red, warm and swollen, and histamine is detected in the tissue fluid. The swelling settles within a short time, and a sample shows no damage to the endothelial cells. Their mast cells carry normal H2 receptors.<br><br>What best explains how the swelling formed, and why it settled quickly?","o":["Endothelial cells contracted to form gaps, then histamine decayed with H2 feedback limiting more release","Endothelial cells were destroyed to form gaps, then new vessels grew back over the following several days","Smooth muscle contracted to narrow vessels, and histamine then stopped because it was blocked by lipoxins","Plasma proteins pulled the vessel wall apart, then fibrin sealed the gaps with a long-term fibrotic scar","Neutrophil integrins opened the vessel wall, then the neutrophils all died within a few minutes of leaving"],"e":"Step 1: histamine relaxes smooth muscle through nitric oxide and contracts endothelial cells, producing gaps of 0.1 to 0.4 μm without damaging the cells. Fluid escape causes swelling, while vasodilation causes redness and warmth. Step 2: this is rapid and short-lived, lasting minutes. Histamine acts immediately but briefly, and H2 receptors on mast cells and basophils stop secretion when histamine is very abundant.","w":["Destruction is the endothelial injury pattern, which may last hours to days, and no damage was found.","Vascular smooth muscle relaxes under histamine, widening rather than narrowing the vessel.","Plasma proteins pass through the gaps rather than making them, and no fibrosis would occur here.","The gaps form through endothelial contraction, and neutrophils survive for a couple of days."],"k":"Histamine leak: endothelial contraction, brief and limited by decay and H2 feedback.","rn":[["","What are the 2 actions of histamine in vessels?","What are the 2 actions of histamine in vessels?","Relaxes smooth muscle; Contracts endothelial cells"],["","Why do endothelial cells contract in inflammation?","Why do endothelial cells contract in inflammation?","Create gaps; Leukocytes, plasma proteins leak out"],["","How wide are the gaps between contracted endothelial cells?","How wide are the gaps between contracted endothelial cells?","0.1 to 0.4 micrometres"]]},{"id":44123,"t":"Acute to Chronic Inflammation","s":"A 48-year-old has a lung lesion with cough over many months and has been unable to clear a persistent pathogen. A biopsy shows crowded macrophages, some resembling epithelial cells and others fused with many nuclei, surrounded by lymphocytes around a necrotic centre. Lung function tests have gradually fallen. Cytokine analysis shows raised interferon gamma.<br><br>What is the process, and what best explains the falling lung function?","o":["Granulomatous inflammation, in which the crowded structure impairs function of this organ","Acute neutrophilic inflammation, in which leaked enzymes alone explain all the months of decline","Septic shock, in which tumour necrosis factor has caused a sudden fall in cardiac output","Complete resolution, in which the macrophages are clearing what remains before normal function returns","An abscess under a healed wound, in which pus has been trapped beneath closed skin"],"e":"Step 1: a chronic course with epithelioid cells, giant cells, lymphocytes and central necrosis around a persistent pathogen indicates granulomatous inflammation. T cells drive prolonged macrophage recruitment, and interferon gamma activates macrophages. Step 2: in highly functional organs, such as the lungs, a granuloma can impair overall function. The persistent inflammatory state itself is harmful.","w":["Months of disease with this cell pattern are not acute neutrophilic inflammation.","Septic shock presents as a severe syndrome with a fall in blood pressure, not this slow picture.","Resolution would show a cleared environment and restored function, not progressive decline.","This lung lesion has macrophages and giant cells rather than pus under closed skin."],"k":"Persistent pathogen → granuloma → impaired function in the lungs.","rn":[["","What is the effect of granulomas in organs like the lung?","What is the effect of granulomas in organs like the lung?","Impair organ function"]]},{"id":44124,"t":"Systemic Inflammation","s":"Two patients are admitted to intensive care with low blood pressure, disseminated intravascular coagulation and metabolic abnormalities. Patient A has a severe bacterial infection spreading from a wound. Patient B has severe burns, and repeated tests show no infection. Both have very high tumour necrosis factor and interleukin 1.<br><br>Which option correctly classifies them and identifies the shared mechanism?","o":["A septic shock, B systemic inflammatory response syndrome; both have overproduced cytokines","A systemic inflammatory response syndrome, B septic shock; both have overproduced cytokines","Both septic shock; both have a resistant bacterial infection at a burn site","A septic shock, B primary chronic inflammation; only A has overproduced cytokines","Both systemic inflammatory response syndrome; neither has raised cytokine levels"],"e":"Step 1: septic shock is due to sepsis from a severe bacterial infection, while systemic inflammatory response syndrome is a similar syndrome from non-infectious causes such as severe burns and trauma. So A has septic shock and B has the non-infectious syndrome. Step 2: overproduction of cytokines, including tumour necrosis factor and interleukin 1, causes the severe pathological events common to both.","w":["This reverses the two classifications.","Patient B has no infection, so this cannot be septic shock.","B has an acute syndrome with raised cytokines, not primary chronic inflammation.","Patient A is infected, and both have raised cytokines."],"k":"Infection divides septic shock from SIRS; cytokine excess unites them.","rn":[["","What is SIRS? Give 2 causes.","What is SIRS? Give 2 causes.","Systemic Inflammatory Response Syndrome; Severe burns; Trauma"]]},{"id":44125,"t":"Systemic Inflammation","s":"A 30-year-old with an infected leg wound has a temperature 2°C above normal, tiredness and raised interleukin 1 and interleukin 6. They take aspirin, and their temperature improves over the next hours. Blood cytokine levels remain high and C-reactive protein stays raised.<br><br>What best explains why the temperature fell while the cytokines did not?","o":["Aspirin blocked prostaglandin synthesis downstream of the cytokines, without stopping their production","Aspirin destroyed the cytokines, but the laboratory test continued to detect their inactive fragments","Aspirin cured the wound infection, but cytokine levels always stay raised for several months afterwards","Aspirin blocked hepatic C-reactive protein synthesis, and this was the only direct cause of the fever","Aspirin blocked selectins, so that fewer neutrophils reached the hypothalamus and released heat there"],"e":"Step 1: endogenous pyrogens such as tumour necrosis factor, interleukin 1 and interleukin 6 induce COX-2, which stimulates prostaglandins, and these act on the hypothalamus to raise the set temperature. Step 2: aspirin inhibits prostaglandin synthesis, interrupting the cascade after the cytokines. The cytokines and the liver's acute phase response can therefore continue, and the source of inflammation is unchanged.","w":["Aspirin inhibits prostaglandin synthesis and does not destroy cytokines.","Fever reduction does not show that the underlying infection has been cured.","C-reactive protein is not the fever mediator, and it remains raised here.","Selectins mediate rolling and are not part of the fever pathway or aspirin's action."],"k":"Aspirin acts downstream of cytokines by blocking prostaglandins.","rn":[["","What does aspirin inhibit?","What does aspirin inhibit?","Prostaglandin synthesis"]]},{"id":44126,"t":"Mediators of Inflammation","s":"A 6-year-old has recurrent bacterial infections. In laboratory testing, antibodies bind normally to the bacteria and C3 is present in plasma, but C3 cannot be split. Their phagocytes have normal receptors. However, the phagocytes engulf the bacteria poorly, and few membrane attack complexes form.<br><br>Which explanation links both findings?","o":["Without C3b, bacteria are not tagged and C5 cleavage falls, reducing C5b","Without C5a, the antibodies cannot bind to the bacteria and all opsonins fail","Without C3a, phagocyte receptors are lost, and the bacteria are protected from lysis","Without selectin, no phagocyte reaches the plasma and complement is never made","Without lipoxin, the cascade is switched off too early and its products are not formed"],"e":"Step 1: cleavage of C3 yields C3a and C3b, and C3b is deposited on pathogens as an opsonin, making them easily identifiable to phagocytes. Its loss explains poor engulfment despite normal phagocyte receptors. Step 2: C3b also participates in cleaving C5 into C5a and C5b, and C5b is part of the membrane attack complex. A single upstream block therefore impairs both functions.","w":["Antibody binding is normal and does not depend on C5a.","C3a is an anaphylatoxin, and the phagocyte receptors are normal here.","Complement factors are made in the liver and are not dependent on selectin.","Lipoxins inhibit neutrophil recruitment and are not the explanation for these findings."],"k":"C3b is upstream of both opsonisation and the membrane attack complex.","rn":[["","What is the job of C3b?","What is the job of C3b?","Cleaves C5 to C5a, C5b; Opsonises pathogens"]]},{"id":44127,"t":"Wound Healing Phases","s":"A 22-year-old returns two weeks after a small penetrating wound to the thigh. The skin over it healed quickly, but now the area is red, hot and swollen with tenderness beneath the closed skin. A fragment of clothing was carried deep into the wound at the time of injury. They are not systemically unwell. No pink granulation tissue is visible at the surface.<br><br>What is the likely process, and what is the best management?","o":["Wound abscess from early top closure; open it and debride the material","Normal repair from the angiofibro stage; leave it, as granulation has finished","Septic shock from bacteria in circulation; give antibiotics alone and leave it closed","Granulomatous inflammation from tuberculosis; stitch the skin to contain the disease","Wound contraction from myofibroblasts; pack the top with compounds to slow healing"],"e":"Step 1: a small wound with damage and retained clothing below, whose top closes before the infection is cleared, is the classic set-up for a wound abscess. The whole cycle repeats, with a new vascular phase making it red, hot and swollen. Step 2: draining takes away the closed-over top and debridement removes the material, making a path for the neutrophil-macrophage alliance. Antibiotics are not front-line here because the patient is not septic.","w":["Red, hot swelling under closed skin is not the picture of completed granulation.","The patient is not systemically unwell, and leaving it closed traps the source.","Nothing points to tuberculosis, and stitching would not remove retained material.","Contraction does not produce these signs, and packing compounds can make wounds worse."],"k":"Top closes over retained material → abscess → drain and debride.","rn":[["","What is the treatment for an abscess?","What is the treatment for an abscess?","Drain it; Debride the wound"],["","How does a wound abscess form?","How does a wound abscess form?","Top heals first; Bacteria multiply in trapped debris"]]},{"id":44128,"t":"Wound Healing Phases","s":"A 70-year-old with poor nutrition and poor blood supply to a foot develops an infected wound. Over three days, the area spreads and they become very unwell with fever and low blood pressure. Only a few neutrophils are found at the wound edge. Blood tests show that bacteria have entered the circulation.<br><br>What happened in the fight at the wound, and what is the resulting condition?","o":["Bacteria won because few troops arrived with little ammunition; this is sepsis","Neutrophils won because few cells were needed; this is normal progress to repair","A long-term draw formed because the troops held the line; this is a granuloma","Macrophages won because blood supply was low; this is an expected wound contraction","Fibroblasts lost because vessels were blocked; this is a local abscess only"],"e":"Step 1: poor blood supply means enough troops cannot be transported to the front, and low nutrition means neutrophils have little ammunition, so bacteria can win. Step 2: when bacteria overwhelm defences and reach the circulation, they trigger a systemic inflammatory response and serious illness, often called sepsis. This is one of the situations for which antibiotics are indicated.","w":["The spread, shock and bacteria in the blood show defences have failed.","A granuloma develops over weeks to months and does not fit this rapid course.","Wound contraction is the final healing stage rather than this acute decline.","Fibroblasts are not fighters, and the circulating bacteria show this is more than a local abscess."],"k":"Weak delivery and low nutrition → bacteria win → sepsis.","rn":[["","What happens if bacteria win?","What happens if bacteria win?","Enter circulation; Systemic inflammatory response (sepsis)"]]},{"id":44129,"t":"Wound Healing Phases","s":"A 55-year-old has had a chronic inflammatory condition for years. Tests show persistently raised tumour necrosis factor and interleukin 1, with raised C-reactive protein. They now have reduced cardiac output, a recent thrombus and evidence of insulin resistance in skeletal muscle. The joints that were originally inflamed have remained the main local problem.<br><br>What best explains these distant problems?","o":["Persistent cytokines, helpful in the short term, are now harming distant organs","Leaked neutrophil enzymes from the joints have directly digested the heart and muscle","Liver failure has stopped C-reactive protein production, which now causes the thrombus","Histamine released from skin mast cells has caused the drop in cardiac output","Lipoxin overproduction has switched off every protective acute phase response"],"e":"Step 1: the raised cytokines and C-reactive protein with years of disease indicate persistent systemic inflammation, which can follow chronic inflammation. Step 2: although fever, more liver proteins and more white cells are helpful in the short term, persistent tumour necrosis factor causes low cardiac output and thrombus formation. With interleukin 1 it also causes insulin resistance in multiple tissues, including skeletal muscle.","w":["Leaked enzymes cause nearby tissue damage rather than these distant cytokine effects.","C-reactive protein is raised, so production has clearly not stopped.","Histamine is fast and short-lived, so it does not explain years of distant damage.","Lipoxins inhibit neutrophil recruitment and do not explain these distant cytokine effects."],"k":"Persistent TNF and IL-1 harm heart, vessels and muscle metabolism.","rn":[["","What happens in a short-term draw?","What happens in a short-term draw?","Macrophage-neutrophil alliance pushes debris out slowly; Repair phase begins behind them"]]},{"id":44130,"t":"Wound Healing Phases","s":"A 40-year-old has a clean abdominal operation under aseptic conditions, with the wound stitched closed. A second patient has a deep contaminated wound left open and full of dead tissue. Biopsies at the same time point show few neutrophils with early repair in the first, and masses of neutrophils and debris in the second.<br><br>What best explains the difference, and what should the second patient receive first?","o":["The first has nothing to fight; the second needs debridement to tip the balance","The first has no inflammation at all; the second needs compounds packed into it","The first has an abscess forming; the second needs stitches placed across the crater","The first has lost its fibroblasts; the second needs immediate closure over its debris","The first is septic; the second needs only a dressing kept over the scab"],"e":"Step 1: aseptic surgery introduces no bacteria, and stitches approximate the edges. Neutrophils arrive, find nothing to fight, and the process moves almost at once to stages 3 and 4. Step 2: the contaminated wound has a real fight, and dead material favours bacteria. Debridement physically removes it, tipping the balance in the alliance's favour, and it is the most effective intervention.","w":["The surgical wound still heals through inflammation, though much less, and packing can worsen a wound.","The first shows early repair rather than an abscess, and stitching a dirty crater would trap debris.","Fibroblasts are present in repair, and closing over debris is the set-up for an abscess.","The first shows no sign of sepsis, and the scab is the least important part of a wound."],"k":"Aseptic wounds shortcut phases; contaminated wounds need debridement."}]);
